<div class="titlepage"><div class="t">THE COUNTRY BETWEEN</div><div class="s">an allegory</div>
<div class="a">Matt Blair</div><div class="proof">PROOF COPY — NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION</div>
<div class="meta">Complete first draft · 63 sections · approx. 26,000 words</div></div>
<div class="partpage"><h1>PART ONE</h1><h2>The Sundering</h2></div>
<h3 class="sec">One</h3>
<p class="p">The road was gone in the morning.</p>
<p class="p">Not blocked. Steadfast had seen the road blocked, twice, and both times there had been something to look at — a slide of rock, a fallen tree, a place where a river had decided differently. This was not that. He came out of the lodging with his pack on and his boots laced and looked at the place where the road had been for the nineteen years he had walked it, and there was grass there. Old grass. Grass that had been growing a long time.</p>
<p class="p">He stood in the doorway.</p>
<p class="p">Then he walked out to where it should have been and put his boot on the ground and pressed, as though the road were under the turf and could be found by pushing. He went twenty paces north and tried again. He went forty paces south. He got down and put his hand flat on the grass and felt nothing but grass, and cold, and the small movement of something living underneath.</p>
<p class="p">The keeper of the lodging came out and stood with him and said nothing.</p>
<p class="d">— It was here, Steadfast said.</p>
<p class="d">— I know it was.</p>
<p class="d">— I walked it in.</p>
<p class="d">— I know you did. I watched you come.</p>
<p class="d">— Then where is it.</p>
<p class="p">The keeper looked out at the grass with the expression of a man reading something in a language he only half has. — I have seen this four times, he said. Not here. Elsewhere. It is not the road that moved.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast waited.</p>
<p class="d">— It is the country, the keeper said. The country has turned underneath it. There is no walking back to a thing that is still there.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Two</h3>
<p class="p">He had been Steadfast for eight of the nineteen years and had been given it, which is the only way anybody gets one.</p>
<p class="p">Before that he had been called something ordinary that he had got from his mother and had never thought about. What happened was that there was a winter with a lot of weather in it and a stretch of the road above the ford went bad, and he had walked it anyway, four times, in both directions, carrying for people who could not, and at the end of it a woman at the staging house had said it in front of about nine people and it had stuck within a season.</p>
<p class="p">He had been forty-one. He remembered being pleased in a way he was slightly ashamed of and going out afterwards and standing in the cold with his ears hot.</p>
<p class="p">And then it had been his, and he had lived inside it, and it had turned out to be a good name to live inside because it told you what to do. When you did not know what to do you did the steady thing, and everybody knew that was what you would do, and after a few years you did not have to decide.</p>
<p class="p">He was very good at the road.</p>
<p class="p">That is not nothing and he would like it said plainly, because everything that came afterwards has a way of making it sound like it was not. He knew the water on every stretch between the ford and the high crossing. He knew which of the lodging-keepers watered the beer and which of them would sit up with a sick man. He knew the six places where people came off it in the first fortnight and why, and he could tell in about an hour of walking with someone whether they were going to last, and he was right about it more often than anybody he ever met.</p>
<p class="p">Nineteen years. He could have said what he was for in a sentence, and did, out loud, to people, without any embarrassment at all.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Three</h3>
<p class="p">He spent nine days looking for it.</p>
<p class="p">That is the part he does not enjoy remembering. He went north along where it had been, and then south, and then he did a thing he was ashamed of afterwards and went out on a bearing at right angles for most of a day on the theory that it might have been <em>displaced</em>, that it might be lying two miles east like a rope kicked off a path, and he stood at the end of that day in a field with cows in it.</p>
<p class="p">He came back to the lodging every night because he did not know what else to do, and the keeper fed him and did not say anything about it, which Steadfast noticed at the time and understood the size of much later.</p>
<p class="p">On the fourth day he found the milestone.</p>
<p class="p">It was where it should have been, on its side in the grass, half sunk, with the figures still legible and moss in them. He got down and cleared it with his hands and read it and it said what it had always said, which was a number and a place, and both of them were still true and neither of them was any use, because there was no road for them to be measured along.</p>
<p class="p">He sat next to it for about an hour.</p>
<p class="p">That was the first time he understood that the trouble was not that something had been taken away. Everything was still there. The stone was there and the ford was there and the high crossing was there and the distances between them had not changed by a yard. What had gone was the thing that had joined them.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Four</h3>
<p class="p">There were about thirty people at the lodging within the fortnight and that was when he made his mistake.</p>
<p class="p">They came in off the country, in ones and twos, the way people do. Some of them had been a day out and some of them a week. They came in with the same face on them, all of them, and they came to him — because of the name, because he was the one who had walked it nineteen years, because a man in a bad hour will look round a room for somebody who has done the thing longest.</p>
<p class="p">And he told them it would come back.</p>
<p class="p">He did not say it exactly like that. He was too careful for that and had been careful for nineteen years. What he said was that in his experience the road went bad in patches and came good, and that he had walked the stretch above the ford through a winter when everybody said it was finished, and that the sensible thing was to stay put and keep together and not go off in weather on somebody's guess.</p>
<p class="p">Every word of that was true and every word of it was about a different thing, and he knew that within about ten days and went on saying it for six weeks.</p>
<p class="p">He has thought about why, and the answer is not complicated and does not improve with looking at it. He said it because they were frightened and it steadied them. He said it because saying it steadied him. And he said it because he was the man who knew, and there is no way to stand in a room full of people who need you to know and say <em>I have no idea</em> that does not cost you the only thing you have ever been.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Five</h3>
<p class="p">They went anyway, over the winter, in the way that a thing empties rather than ends.</p>
<p class="p">Two of them went south in the first month and were sensible about it and said so and asked him to come. Four went to look for the high crossing. A family went back east to a place they had come from. One man, who was not well, went out on his own in a hard week in the second month and did not come back and was not found, and there is nothing to say about that except that Steadfast had told him the day before to sit tight and wait.</p>
<p class="p">By the thaw there were nine of them left and none of the nine believed him.</p>
<p class="p">Nobody was cruel about it. That was somehow the worst part — there was no scene, nobody stood up and said <em>you have been telling us this for four months and you do not know</em>, and he sometimes wished afterwards that somebody had, because it would have been quicker. What happened instead was that they stopped asking him things. He noticed it over about three weeks. He would come into the room and a conversation would go on rather than stopping and turning to him, and it took him some time to work out that this was not rudeness.</p>
<p class="p">He was not the man who knew. He had not been for months. They had all understood it before he had, and had gone on being kind to him while he caught up.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Six</h3>
<p class="p">The map came in the spring, with a man who had six of them.</p>
<p class="p">He was pleasant and unhurried and did not oversell it. He spread one on the table at the lodging and it was beautiful — inked in three colours on good hide, with a scale, and a compass rose in the corner drawn by somebody who could draw, and the road on it as a double line running north and south, confident, unbroken, with the lodging-houses along it as little squares.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast looked for a long time at the square with his own lodging's name on it.</p>
<p class="d">— Have you seen it, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— I have people out.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not the question I asked.</p>
<p class="p">The man did not take offence, which was somehow worse. He said, in the same unhurried way, that no one man could walk all of it, that this was what surveying was, that he marked the stretches he was less sure of in a paler hand — look — and that he would rather sell an honest map with three doubtful sections on it than no map at all, because people were going out on nothing.</p>
<p class="p">And that, Steadfast thought, was true. That was the trouble with all of it. It was true.</p>
<p class="p">He did not buy the map. Two of the nine did.</p>
<p class="p">He watched them go with it a week later and stood at the door of the lodging and did not say one word to either of them about what he had stood in on a cold morning in the doorway behind him, because he had spent four months telling people things that steadied them and had run out of any appetite for the sound of his own voice being certain.</p>
<p class="p">That is the whole of the reason. He would like it to have been a better one.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Seven</h3>
<p class="p">Three people asked him to come with them and he said no to all three.</p>
<p class="p">The two who went south in the first month asked him honestly and he said no because he still believed the road would come back and thought them foolish for going, and that was the only one of the three he does not blame himself for, because at least he had a reason he believed at the time.</p>
<p class="p">The family going east asked in a general way, at a meal, in front of the children, and he said no in a general way, and later thought that the woman had meant it and had asked in front of the children so that it would be easier to accept.</p>
<p class="p">And the keeper asked him, at the end, in the summer, when there were four of them left and the lodging was going to close.</p>
<p class="d">— Come with us, he said. — My brother has a place at the coast. There is room and there is work and it is a long way from any of this.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— Why not.</p>
<p class="p">And Steadfast could not answer him, and stood there, and the keeper waited a long time — longer than was comfortable, long enough that Steadfast understood afterwards that it had been on purpose — and then said:</p>
<p class="d">— All right. Then take some food.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Eight</h3>
<p class="p">He went west because of the milestone.</p>
<p class="p">That is the honest reason and it is not a good one. The figures on the stone were a number and a place and the place was west, and it had been the last true thing he had touched, and when the lodging closed and there was nowhere to be, he found that his feet had an opinion.</p>
<p class="p">He knew what was west. Everybody did. There was settled country for a while and then there was not, and then there was a stretch of stone and grey scrub that had a name in the way weather has a name, and nobody crossed it because there was nothing on the other side worth crossing to, and the people who had gone into it were people who were not coming back.</p>
<p class="p">He walked for nine days through country where there were still farms.</p>
<p class="p">He was fed twice and turned away once and slept in a barn with the owner's knowledge and in two more without it. On the ninth day he passed a boy on a gate who asked him where he was going and he said west and the boy said — there is nothing west — and he said — I know — and walked on, and heard the boy call something after him that he did not turn round for.</p>
<p class="p">Then the farms stopped.</p>
<p class="p">There was no border. He noticed the ground going hard under him over about two days, and the hedges giving out, and then one morning he came up out of a dip and there was grey scrub in front of him as far as he could see in every direction, low, ankle-high, going nowhere.</p>
<p class="p">He stood at the edge of it for most of an hour.</p>
<p class="p">Then he stepped into it, and that was the whole of the ceremony.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Nine</h3>
<p class="p">Four months.</p>
<p class="p">He would come to say it in months for a long time after he should have stopped, and there is not a great deal to say about them, which is itself the truest thing about that country.</p>
<p class="p">He learned what it had in it. Stone. Scrub that broke like old bread. Water in seams if you knew to look at the underside of a slope, and he did not know, for the first three weeks, and nearly died of not knowing, and then met a man who showed him in about four minutes and would not stay to be thanked.</p>
<p class="p">He tried to make fire twice. Both times he got a smell like scorched hair and a handful of ash that blew off before it was cool, and the second time he sat and looked at his hands for a while and then packed up and went on.</p>
<p class="p">He met fourteen people in four months. Every one of them told him their account inside a quarter of an hour, usually before he asked — where they had come off, how long they had been out, what they had been before — and he understood that this was what people did out there, and did it himself, and heard himself doing it, and carried on doing it because there was nothing else to give anybody.</p>
<p class="p">They asked his name a dozen times and he said it a dozen times.</p>
<p class="p">That was the part he could not have explained to anybody, then or afterwards. Not the thirst, not the ground, not the cold. That a dozen times in four months a stranger in a grey country asked a man what he was called, and a man who had not been steadfast about one single thing since a morning in a doorway heard himself say the word out loud, in the ordinary way, like a fact, and got away with it.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Ten</h3>
<p class="p">On the last day of it he smelled smoke.</p>
<p class="p">He was four months in by then and had stopped expecting anything, which is a thing that happens to a man's face before it happens to his mind, and he stopped where he was and turned his head and could not credit it, because there was nothing in that country that would take a flame and hold it. He had tried. He knew.</p>
<p class="p">He stood for a while and it did not go away.</p>
<p class="p">So he went towards it — not quickly, because he had learned by then what quickly cost, and not with any hope in particular, because hope was not a thing he had carried in some weeks. He went towards it the way you go towards a sound in the night: to find out, and to stop wondering.</p>
<p class="p">It took him the rest of the afternoon.</p>
<p class="p">And at the end of it there was a fold in the ground he had to come up over, and he came up over it, and stopped.</p>
<div class="partpage"><h1>PART TWO</h1><h2>The Waste</h2></div>
<h3 class="sec">Eleven</h3>
<p class="p">It was a garden.</p>
<p class="p">That was the thing he could not get past, standing at the edge of it with his hand still on the strap of his pack. Beans up canes. Something low and green in rows, four rows, with the soil between them dark and turned. A wall of stacked flat stones about waist high running all the way round, which someone had carried there one at a time from somewhere he could not see. And at the back of it a house, a real one, with a roof and a chimney and a door.</p>
<p class="p">A door. In the Waste. A thing that could be shut.</p>
<p class="p">The wall was not finished. It ran three sides and stopped, and the fourth side was open to the scrub with a line of stones laid out along it, spaced, waiting, like something interrupted.</p>
<p class="d">— You can come in, she said, without turning round. — You have been standing there a while.</p>
<p class="p">She was at the far end of the rows with her back to him, doing something to the beans. He had made no sound he was aware of.</p>
<p class="p">He came in through the gap in the wall because there was no gate, and the ground under his boots changed, and that was what undid him — not the green, not the smoke. The ground. It had been worked. It gave slightly. He had been walking on stone for four months and had stopped noticing until it stopped.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">She fed him.</p>
<p class="p">She did not ask him anything until he had eaten, and she did not watch him eat. He ate badly and fast and had to stop twice. There was a stew of something with beans in it and there was bread, and the bread was the thing that broke his composure, though he got through it without her seeing.</p>
<p class="p">Then she sat back and looked at him with her hands round a cup.</p>
<p class="d">— How long have you been out?</p>
<p class="d">— Four months.</p>
<p class="d">— Four months, she said.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes.</p>
<p class="d">— You still say it in months.</p>
<p class="p">She said it without any cruelty, the way you would tell a man he had cut himself and not noticed, and Steadfast sat in her warm house with her food in him and felt the floor go.</p>
<p class="d">— What do they call you?</p>
<p class="p">He had been asked that a dozen times in four months and had answered it a dozen times without thinking, and he opened his mouth to do it again and could not get the word out in front of her.</p>
<p class="p">She waited.</p>
<p class="d">— Steadfast, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— Is that yours?</p>
<p class="d">— It was given.</p>
<p class="d">— By people who had watched you do something.</p>
<p class="d">— Nineteen years of it.</p>
<p class="p">She nodded, and did not smile, which he had been afraid she was going to do.</p>
<p class="d">— All right, she said. — Steadfast.</p>
<p class="p">She used it after that in the ordinary way, every day, for everything. Pass me that, Steadfast. The light is going, Steadfast. He flinched at it for weeks and she never once made anything of the flinch.</p>
<p class="d">— How long have you been here, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— Eleven years.</p>
<p class="p">He waited for the rest of it, the account, the how and the why, because everyone he had met in four months had given him theirs within a quarter of an hour, usually before he had asked. She drank her tea.</p>
<p class="d">— Eleven years, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes.</p>
<p class="d">— And you have not —</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="p">She put the cup down and stood and started clearing, and he understood that the conversation was not being refused, exactly. It was being deferred to a time when it would be less interesting to both of them.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Twelve</h3>
<p class="p">He meant to stay two nights.</p>
<p class="p">He said so, that first evening, and she said that was fine and showed him where he could sleep, which was a lean-to on the north side of the house with a straw mattress and a blanket that smelled of the smoke. He lay in it and listened to nothing — the Waste made no sound at night, no insects, no wind in anything — and in the morning she was already out and there was porridge on the stove and a note of the kind you leave for somebody you are not worried about.</p>
<p class="p"><em>Water in the barrel. Do not use the second barrel.</em></p>
<p class="p">He used the first barrel. Then he stood in the doorway and looked at her working in the rows and could not think of a reason to leave that day rather than the next.</p>
<p class="p">By the fourth morning she put him on the wall.</p>
<p class="d">— Can you lay stone?</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— Good. Neither could I.</p>
<p class="p">She showed him. It was less like building than like a puzzle with no picture: you put your hand on a stone and you did not pick it up yet, you turned it where it lay and looked at the face it presented, and if the face was wrong you left it and tried another, and the whole art of it was in not lifting anything until you were sure. Lifting was the expensive part. She could tell from six feet whether a stone would seat.</p>
<p class="p">He was terrible at it. He lifted everything. By noon his back had a hot wire in it and his section had a bulge in the middle where he had forced two stones to sit together that did not want to.</p>
<p class="d">— That will come down, she said, going past with a bucket.</p>
<p class="d">— I can take it out.</p>
<p class="d">— No. Leave it. It will come down on its own or it will not, and either way you will learn more from watching it than from me telling you.</p>
<p class="p">It did not come down. He watched it for days, expecting it, and it stood, ugly, out of line with everything on either side of it, a bulge in an otherwise honest wall.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">The water was the thing he could not work out.</p>
<p class="p">There was no well. He looked. There was no spring, no seam in the rock anywhere within an hour of the house, and he walked the ground for an hour in every direction on the sixth day, partly to see and partly because he had begun to want a reason to be useful and had not found one.</p>
<p class="p">He came back and she was sitting on the step.</p>
<p class="d">— There is no water here, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— Then where.</p>
<p class="p">She got up and took him round the back of the house, and it was so simple he was angry about it for an hour afterwards. The roof was pitched to a gutter of split wood. The gutter ran to the barrel. Beside the barrel was the second barrel, the one he was not to use, and beside that a run of flat stones set into the ground sloping to a sunk basin lined with clay.</p>
<p class="d">— It rains eleven days a year here, she said. — Sometimes fourteen.</p>
<p class="d">— Eleven days.</p>
<p class="d">— You have to be very good at eleven days.</p>
<p class="p">He looked at the gutter, which was old and had been repaired in four places, each repair a different wood.</p>
<p class="d">— And the second barrel.</p>
<p class="d">— Is for when I have got the first one wrong.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Thirteen</h3>
<p class="p">He asked about the road on the ninth day. He had been building up to it and made a poor job of the approach.</p>
<p class="d">— When you came out. Did yours go the same way, or —</p>
<p class="d">— Steadfast.</p>
<p class="d">— I only mean, if it was the same, then someone must —</p>
<p class="d">— Steadfast.</p>
<p class="p">She was kneeling in the second row with her hands in the dirt and she did not stop what she was doing.</p>
<p class="d">— I am not going to talk about the road.</p>
<p class="d">— Why not.</p>
<p class="d">— Because you want me to help you find it, and I cannot, and after we have talked about it for a week you will be angry with me for not being able to. I would rather skip that part. I have done it before and it always goes that way.</p>
<p class="p">She pulled something and shook the soil off it and set it in the basket.</p>
<p class="d">— How many times, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— You are the ninth.</p>
<p class="p">That stopped him. He had been thinking of himself as a weather event.</p>
<p class="d">— What happened to the other eight.</p>
<p class="d">— Six went on. Two went back. One of the two who went back came through again about a year later going the other way and did not stop, and I have thought about that one more than the other eight together.</p>
<p class="d">— Why.</p>
<p class="d">— Because he saw the smoke, she said. — And chose not to come and stand at my wall. And I have never worked out whether that was strength or the other thing, and I would like to know, and I never will.</p>
<p class="p">She sat back on her heels then and looked at him properly for the first time since the meal.</p>
<p class="d">— That is the last question about the road you get for a while. Have it back if you want to spend it differently.</p>
<p class="d">— No, he said. — That was a good one.</p>
<p class="d">— It was, she said, and went back to the row.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Fourteen</h3>
<p class="p">The visitor came in the third week.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast saw him first, from the wall, and did not recognise what he was looking at for a moment because the man was moving so slowly. He came across the scrub from the north in a diagonal, stopping often, not the stop of a man resting but the stop of a man who has forgotten mid-stride what he is doing and has to wait for it to come back.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve was beside him before he had said anything.</p>
<p class="d">— Fill the pot, she said. — Not the good bread. The other one.</p>
<p class="d">— Do you know him?</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="p">She went out to meet him past the line of laid stones, and Steadfast watched her do a thing he thought about for a long time afterwards. She did not help him. She walked out and stopped six feet short of him and stood, and said something he could not hear, and waited. The man said something. She waited again. It went on like that for a while, the two of them standing in the open with a gap between them that she did not close, and then the man came forward on his own feet and she turned and walked in beside him without touching him.</p>
<p class="p">The man ate. He did not talk much and what he said did not join up. He had been a builder, or his father had. He had a daughter. There was a thing about a horse that started twice and did not finish either time. Grieve asked him nothing except whether he wanted more, four times, and each time he did.</p>
<p class="p">He slept in the lean-to and Steadfast slept on the floor by the stove and did not mind.</p>
<p class="p">In the morning the man was gone. He had taken nothing, which Steadfast checked, and was ashamed of checking.</p>
<p class="d">— Will he be all right?</p>
<p class="d">— No, she said.</p>
<p class="d">— Then why did you not —</p>
<p class="d">— Say it.</p>
<p class="d">— Make him stay.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve was scraping the pot out and she stopped and held it against her hip.</p>
<p class="d">— Because he did not ask, she said. — And because the last time I made someone stay who had not asked, he stayed four years and it was not a kindness, it was me being unable to watch someone walk off into it. He hated me by the end and he was right to. Now I feed them and I let them go and I am wrong about half of them and I do not know which half.</p>
<p class="p">She went back to the pot.</p>
<p class="d">— That is the honest answer. There is a nicer one and I could give it to you but you would use it later on someone who deserves the honest one.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Fifteen</h3>
<p class="p">The rain came at the end of the fifth week.</p>
<p class="p">She was up before it, in the dark, and he woke to her moving and came out and found her on the roof with a rag in each hand doing something to the gutter joints by feel. There were no clouds he could see. The sky was the same nothing it always was.</p>
<p class="d">— How do you know.</p>
<p class="d">— My hip. And the beans go quiet. Get the second barrel round.</p>
<p class="p">It rained for nine hours. He had not seen rain in four months and had forgotten that it made noise, and the noise on the roof was so loud they had to raise their voices in the house. The gutter took it. The first barrel filled in two hours and the run-off went down the flat stones to the basin and the basin filled and the overflow from that went out along a channel she had cut into the rows so the water walked itself down between the beans.</p>
<p class="p">She stood in the doorway with her arms folded, wet to the shoulder, and watched the channel work, and she was so plainly happy that he had to look away.</p>
<p class="p">Around the fourth hour a joint went on the north side and the water started coming off the roof in a sheet instead of into the gutter, and she was up the ladder before he had the door open. He went up after her. It was two of them on a wet roof with a bad joint and no light, and she showed him where to put his hands and he put them there and they held it, physically held the wood together, for the better part of an hour until the rain slackened enough for her to get a wedge in.</p>
<p class="p">They came down black with wet. She was laughing. It was the first time he had heard it.</p>
<p class="d">— That is eleven days, she said. — That was one of them.</p>
<p class="p">Then she looked at the wall, at the ugly bulged section he had built, running with water, standing.</p>
<p class="d">— Huh, she said.</p>
<p class="p">That was all. She went in to get dry.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Sixteen</h3>
<p class="p">He stopped counting in months somewhere in the second season and did not notice until she said something.</p>
<p class="p">They were sat on the step at the end of a day. The light in the Waste went orange about an hour before it went, and it was the only time the country was anything other than grey, and they had got into the habit of being outside for it without having discussed it.</p>
<p class="d">— You have been here a while now, she said.</p>
<p class="d">— Since the rain.</p>
<p class="d">— No. Before that.</p>
<p class="d">— Since a while before that, he said.</p>
<p class="p">She smiled at the middle distance.</p>
<p class="d">— There, she said.</p>
<p class="d">— There what.</p>
<p class="d">— Nothing. You did it right.</p>
<p class="p">He worked it out about a minute later and sat with it. He did not feel that he had arrived anywhere. He felt as though something had been taken from him quietly while he was looking at the beans.</p>
<p class="d">— Is that good, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— It is neither.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not an answer.</p>
<p class="d">— No, she said. — It is not.</p>
<p class="p">And then, because he had learned by then that she gave you the real thing if you let the silence run rather than filling it, he let the silence run.</p>
<p class="d">— It happens, she said eventually. — To everyone who stays past the first winter.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not an answer either.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="p">The light went. She got up and knocked the dirt off the back of her skirt.</p>
<p class="d">— The wall, she said. — Tomorrow. I want the fourth side up before the cold, and you are quicker now, and you still lift everything.</p>
<p class="d">— I do not lift everything.</p>
<p class="d">— You lift everything.</p>
<p class="p">She went in. He sat a minute longer in the dark and found that he was thinking about the fourth side of the wall and what stones were near it and how many days it would take them, and that he had not thought about the road since some morning he could not now identify.</p>
<p class="p">He noticed that.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Seventeen</h3>
<p class="p">The cold came, and the garden went to almost nothing, and he learned that this was normal and that she had been feeding him out of a store he had not known existed.</p>
<p class="p">It was under the floor. She lifted three boards by the stove and there was a pit lined with flat stones, and in it beans dried on the cane, and roots in sand, and a hard grey cheese she had made from nothing he could identify and would not discuss.</p>
<p class="d">— How long does that last.</p>
<p class="d">— Until it does not.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not —</p>
<p class="d">— It lasts to the middle of the cold if I am the only one, she said, and put the boards back down.</p>
<p class="p">He stood there while she swept the dust back over the joins with her foot.</p>
<p class="d">— How long with two.</p>
<p class="d">— Steadfast.</p>
<p class="d">— How long with two.</p>
<p class="d">— We will find out, she said. — Which is not the same as short. Go and get the grey wood in before it gets damp.</p>
<p class="p">He went and got the grey wood in. He was angry for two days in a way he could not have justified to anybody, least of all himself, because she had never once suggested he go, and because he had eaten from that pit every day for four months without asking a single question about where any of it came from.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">They worked the fourth side through the cold.</p>
<p class="p">It went slowly, because the stone had to come from further out each week and because the ground was hard and the days were short. He got better. He stopped lifting everything. Somewhere in there he found that he could stand over a stone with his hands at his sides and know, and the knowing arrived without ceremony, the way you notice you have learned a road.</p>
<p class="p">She noticed before he did.</p>
<p class="d">— You did not lift that.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— Hm, she said, and went back to her own end.</p>
<p class="p">He was absurdly pleased for the rest of the day and told himself he was not.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Eighteen</h3>
<p class="p">The fourth side was two stones from finished on a grey afternoon when he understood what finishing it meant.</p>
<p class="p">He was carrying, and she was seating, and there was a gap the width of a forearm left in the line, and he set the stone down beside it rather than handing it over and stood up and looked at the whole thing. Four sides. A closed shape on the ground with a house in it.</p>
<p class="d">— What happens after this.</p>
<p class="p">She did not look up. — After what.</p>
<p class="d">— After the wall.</p>
<p class="d">— The gutter needs doing before the rain. And the second row is finished, so that comes out and goes over.</p>
<p class="d">— No, he said. — After.</p>
<p class="p">She sat back on her heels and wiped her wrist across her forehead and left dirt there, and looked at the gap.</p>
<p class="d">— There is always something.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not what I asked.</p>
<p class="d">— I know what you asked, she said.</p>
<p class="p">She got up. She did not take the stone from him. She went and got the bucket and started away toward the house with it and then stopped about six feet off, which he noticed, because it was the distance she had stood from the man in the autumn.</p>
<p class="d">— You have been asking me that since the first week, she said. — You have just been asking it about the road.</p>
<p class="p">Then she went in, and he stood in the gap in his own wall with a stone in his hands and could not work out whether he had been understood or dismissed, and found that he wanted very badly for it to be dismissed, because the other thing was worse.</p>
<p class="p">He did not put the stone in. He left the gap.</p>
<p class="p">She did not mention it. It stayed open through the cold, one forearm's width, and they both walked past it every day and neither of them said anything about it at all, and that was the first thing they had that was not spoken about.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Nineteen</h3>
<p class="p">The girl came in the thaw.</p>
<p class="p">She was young, which was the first thing, and she was not far gone, which was the second. She came up out of the fold at a walk, not a stagger. She had a pack that was still packed. She had been out twelve days.</p>
<p class="d">— Twelve days, Grieve said. — You are quick.</p>
<p class="d">— I ran the first three.</p>
<p class="d">— Everyone does.</p>
<p class="p">She ate like someone who had not yet learned to be ashamed of eating, and she talked, and what she said joined up. Her name was Ash. She had been on the road six years. She was going to cross the Waste and get to the other side of it, where she believed there was something, and she asked them both, straight out, across the table, whether they knew what was over there.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve said no.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast said no, and heard how his own voice did it — flat, quick, closing — and did not like the sound.</p>
<p class="p">Ash slept in the lean-to. Steadfast lay by the stove and listened to the nothing and did not sleep much.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">In the morning she was up before either of them, packed, standing out by the gap in the fourth side with her boots on.</p>
<p class="d">— You are going, Grieve said, from the door.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes.</p>
<p class="d">— Which way.</p>
<p class="d">— That way, said Ash, and pointed at the flat grey nothing to the east, and she said it the way you would name a street.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve nodded and went and got a cloth bundle from inside and brought it out and put it in the girl's hands. Bread and the hard cheese and a stoppered flask.</p>
<p class="d">— Two days of it, she said. — Do not drink the flask on the first day no matter what you tell yourself about the second.</p>
<p class="d">— Thank you.</p>
<p class="d">— Go well.</p>
<p class="p">And she stepped back.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast said: — Wait.</p>
<p class="p">Both of them looked at him.</p>
<p class="p">He had not known he was going to say it. He heard it come out and heard it land and there was a silence of the kind that has weight, and Grieve did not move at all, which was her giving him the chance to make it a smaller sentence.</p>
<p class="p">He did not take it.</p>
<p class="d">— It is fifteen days across at the narrowest and you are not carrying fifteen. There is nothing east. I came from the east. There is nothing there but more of it, and you will be four days out with an empty flask and no shade and no —</p>
<p class="d">— Steadfast, Grieve said.</p>
<p class="d">— She is nineteen.</p>
<p class="d">— Twenty-two, said Ash.</p>
<p class="d">— You are nineteen or you are twenty-two and either way you are going to die out there, he said, and his voice had gone up and he could not get it down. — Stay until the rains. Stay six weeks. Six weeks and you go with full water and you go knowing where the ground rises, and we can tell you where the ground rises, because she knows and she will tell you if you ask —</p>
<p class="d">— Steadfast.</p>
<p class="d">— Ask her, he said to the girl. — Ask her what is out there. She knows and she will not say it unless you ask, that is how she does it, she will stand six feet off and let you walk into it —</p>
<p class="p">He stopped.</p>
<p class="p">Nobody said anything for a while. A long while. The girl looked at the ground and then at Grieve, and something passed between the two of them that he was not part of and did not have the standing to be part of, and then Ash picked her pack up off the stones.</p>
<p class="d">— I would like the rise, she said to Grieve. — If you know it.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve told her. It took about a minute. Third fold, keep the burnt scrub on your left, do not follow the flat because the flat is longer, there is a seam under the lip of the fourth rise that will give you a cup if you are patient with it.</p>
<p class="p">Ash listened, and repeated the parts back, and went.</p>
<p class="p">They watched her out to the top of the fold and over. She did not turn round.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Twenty</h3>
<p class="p">He waited for it all day and it did not come.</p>
<p class="p">She did the beans. She did the gutter joint that had been on her list since the cold. She asked him at midday to bring the long-handled thing from the lean-to and he brought it, and she said thank you, and that was the whole of it.</p>
<p class="p">By evening he could not stand it.</p>
<p class="d">— Say it.</p>
<p class="d">— Say what.</p>
<p class="d">— That I had no right.</p>
<p class="p">She was scraping the pot. She put it down.</p>
<p class="d">— You had no right, she said. — There. Does that help?</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— No, she said. — It never does.</p>
<p class="p">She sat down across from him and he saw that she was tired in a way she had not been that morning, and understood that she had been carrying it all day too, and had simply been carrying it without making him watch.</p>
<p class="d">— She was going to ask me anyway, she said. — She had it in her hand. She was going to ask me at the gap and I was going to tell her, and she would have gone with it and it would have been hers.</p>
<p class="d">— And now.</p>
<p class="d">— And now she has it because a man shouted. And she took it anyway, which was well done of her, because most of them would not have.</p>
<p class="d">— That is the difference? That is the whole difference? Who says it first?</p>
<p class="d">— Yes, Grieve said.</p>
<p class="d">— That is a very fine distinction to hang somebody's life on.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes, she said. — It is.</p>
<p class="p">And she let that sit, and did not decorate it, and he wanted her to and she would not.</p>
<p class="d">— I have thought about very little else for eleven years, she said. — I have got it wrong in both directions. I have watched two people walk east who I could have stopped by putting my hand out, and I did not put my hand out, and I do not know what happened to either of them and I never will. And I made one man stay four years, and I know exactly what happened to him, because I had to watch it every day at this table.</p>
<p class="p">She turned the cup round.</p>
<p class="d">— The ones I let go, I have to imagine. The one I kept, I do not have to imagine. That is the only reason I do it this way. Not because it is right. Because I have found out which of the two kinds of not-knowing I can carry.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast sat with that.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not a principle, he said at last.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— You said it like a principle. In the autumn. You said it to me like it was a principle and you had worked it out.</p>
<p class="d">— I know.</p>
<p class="d">— Why.</p>
<p class="d">— Because you had been here three weeks, she said, — and you wanted there to be one.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Twenty-One</h3>
<p class="p">They did not talk much for a while after that.</p>
<p class="p">It was not a rupture. That was almost the worst of it — there was no scene to have, nothing to apologise for that had not already been said plainly on the same evening. They worked. They ate. She asked him to pass things and he passed them. Some evenings they sat out for the orange hour the way they had, and it was fine, and it was not the same, and he could not have said what the difference was except that he was now aware of being a person who was in her house.</p>
<p class="p">He thought about Ash constantly and did not mention her once.</p>
<p class="p">He caught himself counting. Fourteen days. Nineteen days. Twenty-six. He did not know what number would mean she was across or what number would mean the other thing, and there was nobody to ask, because the only person who would know was the person he was not going to ask.</p>
<p class="p">On the thirty-first day he came in from the wall and Grieve was sitting at the table doing nothing, which she never did.</p>
<p class="d">— Thirty-one, she said.</p>
<p class="p">He stood in the door.</p>
<p class="d">— You have been counting.</p>
<p class="d">— Since the morning she went.</p>
<p class="p">He came in and sat down. Neither of them said anything about the number or about what it meant or about whether there was a number that meant anything at all.</p>
<p class="p">After a while she got up and put the pot on.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Twenty-Two</h3>
<p class="p">He put the stone in on a still day at the end of the thaw.</p>
<p class="p">He had not planned to. He was moving the last of the far pile and it was in his hands and he was standing at the gap, and there was no reason not to and no ceremony about it, and he turned it twice and found the face and set it in and it seated first time without a sound.</p>
<p class="p">Then he stood back and looked at the wall.</p>
<p class="p">It went all the way round. Four sides, waist high, closed, with the house inside it and the rows inside it and the barrels at the back, and his own section bulging out of the north run like a swallowed thing, ugly, holding.</p>
<p class="p">He waited to feel the thing he had expected to feel.</p>
<p class="p">What came instead was so plain that he had to sit down on the wall he had just finished. There was nothing left to build. There was the gutter, and the rows, and the store under the floor, and the eleven days, and all of it would go round again next year and the year after, and it was good, all of it was good, and it was going to be the same.</p>
<p class="p">He looked at the flat grey nothing to the east where the girl had gone.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve came out with the basket on her hip and saw him sitting on the wall and saw the wall, and stopped.</p>
<p class="p">She did not say well done. She did not say anything for long enough that he looked up at her.</p>
<p class="d">— Right, she said.</p>
<p class="d">— Right.</p>
<p class="d">— That is that, then.</p>
<p class="p">And she went past him to the rows and started work, and did not look at the fourth side again that day, and he sat on the closed wall in the middle of the afternoon and did not move for a long time.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Twenty-Three</h3>
<p class="p">The second year was easier, and that was the problem.</p>
<p class="p">He knew the work now. He knew that the beans went in when the ground took a thumb to the second knuckle and not before, and that the third row drained badly and wanted the shallow end, and that the grey wood had to come in a fortnight earlier than seemed necessary. He stopped being told things. Some time around the middle of it he noticed that she had stopped watching him work, which was the highest thing she had ever said to him and she had not said it.</p>
<p class="p">The rains came late.</p>
<p class="p">He knew they were coming before she did, or at the same time — he could not tell, because when he came out that morning to say so she was already on the roof.</p>
<p class="d">— The joints, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— The north one.</p>
<p class="d">— It will be the north one again.</p>
<p class="d">— It is always the north one.</p>
<p class="p">They did the north joint. Then it rained for nine hours and the gutter took it and the barrel filled in two hours and the run went down the flat stones to the basin and the basin filled and the overflow walked itself down between the rows, and nothing went wrong, and there was nothing for either of them to hold together on a wet roof in the dark.</p>
<p class="p">They stood in the doorway and watched it work.</p>
<p class="d">— Good, she said.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes.</p>
<p class="p">And it was good. That was the whole of it. It was good and it had been good last year and it would be good next year, and he stood there in the doorway of a house that was not his, dry, fed, competent, watching a system he now understood do exactly what he had known it would do.</p>
<p class="p">He did not say much for the rest of that week, and she did not ask.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Twenty-Four</h3>
<p class="p">He found himself keeping a list.</p>
<p class="p">Not on paper. In his head, in the evenings, and it was a list of reasons to be there, and he did not notice he was keeping it until he noticed he was checking it.</p>
<p class="p">The wall was done. That one had gone in the spring.</p>
<p class="p">The store had been dug out and relined the summer before; that one had gone too.</p>
<p class="p">He had cut and hung a proper door for the lean-to, which was the last thing on it of any size, and he had done that in four days at the end of the cold, and it hung well, and every time he went through it now he thought: that is done as well.</p>
<p class="p">By the end of the second year the list was: she is here.</p>
<p class="p">He turned that over on the step one evening while she was inside doing something with the pot, and he found that it did not frighten him the way he expected. What frightened him was the sentence underneath it, which he got to about a week later and which was: <em>and I have stopped asking whether that is a reason.</em></p>
<p class="p">He had walked the King's Road nineteen years. He had known what he was for on every single one of those days, and had been able to say it in a sentence, and had said it to people, out loud, without embarrassment.</p>
<p class="p">He could not now say what he was for. He could say what he did. He could say it in enormous detail — he could tell you the drainage of the third row and the fortnight of the grey wood and the exact face of every stone in eighty feet of wall — and none of it added up to an answer, and the not-adding-up had stopped bothering him some time in the previous year without his permission.</p>
<p class="p">That was the thing that frightened him. Not that the road had gone. That he had stopped looking at the place where it had been.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Twenty-Five</h3>
<p class="p">He asked her at the end of the summer, badly, in the middle of doing something else.</p>
<p class="p">They were lifting the second row, which was finished, and the work was on hands and knees and slow, and he said it into the dirt without looking up because he had worked out that he would not be able to say it to her face.</p>
<p class="d">— Come with me.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve did not stop working.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="p">He sat back on his heels.</p>
<p class="d">— I have not said when.</p>
<p class="d">— No is not about when.</p>
<p class="p">She went on pulling. He watched her hands do it, which he had watched a thousand times, and found that he was angry in the particular way you are angry with somebody who has answered too quickly, even though he knew she had not answered too quickly, she had answered in eleven years.</p>
<p class="d">— You have not thought about it.</p>
<p class="d">— Steadfast.</p>
<p class="d">— One night. Think about it for one night and then say no and I will never —</p>
<p class="d">— I have thought about it every time somebody has come over that fold, she said.</p>
<p class="d">— Nine times. And the answer has been the same nine times and it was not a different question when it was you.</p>
<p class="p">That landed harder than she had meant it to. He saw her see it land.</p>
<p class="d">— That was not — she said, and stopped, and started again. — The answer being the same does not mean the question weighed the same. It weighed more.</p>
<p class="p">She sat up and put her wrists on her knees.</p>
<p class="d">— Ask me why. You have never asked me why. You have asked me about the road and about the water and about what is east, and you have never once asked me why I stay.</p>
<p class="p">He asked her why she stayed.</p>
<p class="d">— The smoke, she said.</p>
<p class="p">He waited for the rest.</p>
<p class="d">— That is it. That is the whole of it. There is nothing here that burns. If I go, in a year there is no smoke over this fold, and the next one who comes out of the south four months in and half dead walks past a mile east of here and never knows he walked past anything.</p>
<p class="d">— Someone else would —</p>
<p class="d">— No, she said. — In eleven years, nobody else has. I have been the only fire in this country for eleven years and I have counted nine people, and if you take the nine off the number and think about how many did not come close enough to see it, that is what I do instead of sleeping.</p>
<p class="p">She said it flatly, without any grandeur at all, the way a woman tells you what her job is.</p>
<p class="p">He said: — Then let me stay.</p>
<p class="p">And she said: — No.</p>
<p class="p">That was the one that took his breath. He had expected a fight about going. He had not prepared anything for this.</p>
<p class="d">— That makes no sense. You have just told me the whole thing runs on somebody being here. I am here. I am good at it now, you have said so, you have —</p>
<p class="d">— I made a man stay once, she said.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not what this is.</p>
<p class="d">— No. It is the other one. He asked and I said yes. I have thought for a long time about which of those two was worse and I have not got anywhere with it, and I am not doing either of them again.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not your decision.</p>
<p class="d">— It is exactly my decision, she said. — You are asking me for a reason to stay. That is what let-me-stay is.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not what I —</p>
<p class="d">— It is. And I will not give you one, because you will take it, and it will hold a year and possibly five, and then one morning you will be standing in that doorway watching the water go down the rows and you will work out that the reason was mine and not yours.</p>
<p class="p">She rubbed the dirt off her palms, one and then the other.</p>
<p class="d">— I have seen that face. I have looked at that face across this table for four years.</p>
<p class="p">She picked up the basket.</p>
<p class="d">— If you stay, stay because you have found something here that is yours. Not because I said the smoke needs two.</p>
<p class="p">Then she took the basket in, and left him on his knees in the second row with the whole afternoon in front of him.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Twenty-Six</h3>
<p class="p">It could not be unasked.</p>
<p class="p">That was the discovery of the following weeks, and it was worse than a quarrel would have been, because there was nothing to repair. She was not cold to him. She did not avoid the subject; twice she raised things adjacent to it herself, easily, to show that it could be raised. They worked. They ate. The orange hour still happened.</p>
<p class="p">But he now knew the answer to a question he had spent two years not asking, and knowing it changed what every ordinary day was. Before, the days had simply been the days. Now each one was a day on which he had chosen to stay, and the choosing was in the room, and it was in the room for her too, and neither of them could put it back.</p>
<p class="p">He caught himself once, in the third week, doing the arithmetic on the store. Two of them, the cold coming, the second year's yield down on the first because of the late rains. He stopped himself and was ashamed, and then a day later did it again.</p>
<p class="p">He knew then that he was going. He did not admit it for a month.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Twenty-Seven</h3>
<p class="p">He told her on a morning that had nothing to recommend it.</p>
<p class="d">— I am going to go.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve was at the stove. She did not turn round straight away, and when she did her face had nothing on it that he could read, and he understood that this was not because she felt nothing but because she had had a month to decide what her face would do.</p>
<p class="d">— When.</p>
<p class="d">— Before the cold. So there is time.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes, she said. — There is time.</p>
<p class="p">She thought.</p>
<p class="d">— Not before the rains. Go after. You want to go out with the seams full behind you, and the fourth rise will hold a cup for a week after and not longer.</p>
<p class="d">— All right.</p>
<p class="d">— And not the flat. Everyone goes on the flat because it is flat.</p>
<p class="d">— Not the flat.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="p">She turned back to the stove.</p>
<p class="p">That was the whole conversation. He had built about nine versions of it over the month and none of them had been that one, and he stood in the middle of the room for a while afterwards with his hands at his sides.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">The rains came and went. He worked the last four weeks harder than he had worked any four weeks in the two years, and she let him, and neither of them said what that was.</p>
<p class="p">He relaid the whole north run of the wall. He did not ask. He took it down in sections and set it properly, and it took eleven days, and when he got to his own piece — the bulge, the two forced stones, the ugly swallowed thing in the middle of eighty feet of honest wall — he stopped with his hands on it.</p>
<p class="d">— Leave that, she said, from the rows. She had not appeared to be watching.</p>
<p class="d">— It is the worst thing here.</p>
<p class="d">— Leave it.</p>
<p class="p">He left it.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Twenty-Eight</h3>
<p class="p">She packed him two days of food.</p>
<p class="p">He watched her do it, standing in the door of the house with his pack already on, and she did it exactly the way she had done it for the girl in the thaw, the same cloth, the same three things, and neither of them said that and both of them knew it, and he had to look at the doorframe for a while.</p>
<p class="p">Bread. The hard cheese. A stoppered flask.</p>
<p class="d">— Two days of it, she said, and put it in his hands. — Do not drink the flask on the first day, no matter what you tell yourself about the second.</p>
<p class="d">— I know.</p>
<p class="d">— I know you know. I am saying it.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes.</p>
<p class="p">She wiped her hands down her front.</p>
<p class="d">— Third fold, keep the burnt scrub on your left. The seam under the lip of the fourth rise will give you a cup if you are patient with it. After that you are past where I know and I have nothing for you.</p>
<p class="p">He knew the words. He had heard them said once already, in this yard, in the thaw, to a girl standing where he was standing with her pack still packed, and he held the bundle and waited for Grieve to say something about that.</p>
<p class="p">She did not.</p>
<p class="p">He could not tell whether she had spared him, or whether there was simply one way out of that country and she had only ever had the one thing to give anybody.</p>
<p class="d">— Thank you, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— There is a line of pale stone about nine days out, if you get that far. Cross it and stop looking behind you, because from there the ground reads differently and looking behind you will put you wrong.</p>
<p class="p">He nodded. He could not do anything else.</p>
<p class="p">They stood there. The wall went all the way round them, waist high, closed, and the smoke went up straight because there was no wind.</p>
<p class="d">— Grieve, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— No, she said. — Not on the step. Whatever it is, it will come out wrong on the step and you will carry the wrong version of it for a year. Go on.</p>
<p class="p">So he went.</p>
<p class="p">He got to the gap that was not a gap any more, the place in the fourth side where the stone had gone in, and had to step over the wall like everyone else, and it was waist high and he was not young and it was undignified, and behind him he heard her make a small sound that might have been a laugh.</p>
<p class="p">He got up onto the fold and did not turn round until the top of it.</p>
<p class="p">She was already back in the rows. She was not watching him go. He had known she would not be and had wanted to check, and having checked, he understood that she had gone back to the rows early on purpose, so that when he looked he would see exactly this, and be able to go.</p>
<p class="d">— Steadfast, she called, without straightening up. — The flask.</p>
<p class="d">— On the second day.</p>
<p class="d">— Good.</p>
<p class="p">And that was the last thing she said to him, and it was about water, and it was the kindest thing anyone had said to him in two years.</p>
<p class="p">He went down the far side of the fold and the smoke went out of sight behind him about a quarter of an hour later, and he stopped once to look for it and could not see it, and after that he did not stop again until the light went.</p>
<div class="partpage"><h1>PART THREE</h1><h2>The Slow Country</h2></div>
<h3 class="sec">Twenty-Nine</h3>
<p class="p">He read the ground for nine days.</p>
<p class="p">He did not admit to himself that this was what he was doing until the fourth morning, when he caught himself squatting at the lip of the third fold going over a patch of scree with his eyes the way you go over a page, and stood up too fast, and was dizzy, and had to put a hand out.</p>
<p class="p">There was nothing. There would not be. Eight months of weather in a country where weather was eleven days a year, and stone that took no print, and a girl who had been quick — she had run the first three, everyone does — so that even on the day she passed there would have been nothing to see.</p>
<p class="p">He looked anyway. At the burnt scrub he looked at the scrub. At the fourth rise he went down on his stomach under the lip and got his cup out of the seam, twenty minutes of it, patient, and while he waited he looked at the ground either side of his own boots for the mark of somebody else's.</p>
<p class="p">On the ninth day he came up over a swell of nothing and found the line of pale stone.</p>
<p class="p">It was not a wall and it was not a road. It was a seam of some whiter rock running out of the north and away south further than he could see, a hand's width proud of the ground in places and buried in others, and he stood on it with a foot either side and understood that he had been told about this and that being told about it was the last thing he had.</p>
<p class="p"><em>Cross it and stop looking behind you, because from there the ground reads differently and looking behind you will put you wrong.</em></p>
<p class="p">He crossed it. He did not stop looking behind him, because she had not said he would be able to, only that it would put him wrong, and it did.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Thirty</h3>
<p class="p">He talked to her for about a fortnight. Not aloud at first. He would come to a slope and think <em>she would go round that</em> and go round it, and be pleased, and then catch the pleasure and find that there was nobody to be pleased at him. He narrated the water to her. He explained his own decisions to her and she agreed with them, which was how he knew he was making her up, because she had never simply agreed with him about anything in two years.</p>
<p class="p">Then it went aloud, and he heard himself, and stopped, and the stopping was worse than the talking.</p>
<p class="p">After that he did the work of walking and nothing else. He filled at seams. He rationed the way she had taught him and caught himself, on the fourth day past the pale stone, holding a flask against the light to read the level the way she held a jar, at the same angle, with the same tilt of the head, and stood there in the middle of a plain feeling like a man wearing another man's coat.</p>
<p class="p">He did not stop doing it. He could not think of a better way to hold a flask.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">The ground did read differently.</p>
<p class="p">He could not have said when it started. There was no morning where he stood in one country and looked back at another. The scrub simply began to come in patches instead of everywhere, and then the patches began to have other things in them, low and grey-green and tough, and one afternoon he went down on his knees to look at something because it was flowering, badly, four small dirty flowers on a stem, the first flower he had seen outside four rows of a garden in two years.</p>
<p class="p">He did not know its name. He picked one and it came apart in his fingers.</p>
<p class="p">Two days later he crossed a watercourse. It was dry. But it had been cut by something, and cut deep, and it ran east to west across his path with a lip on both sides, and he stood in the bottom of it looking at the strata and thought: this runs for part of the year.</p>
<p class="p">He got out the other side and walked on and it took him until evening to work out what was wrong with him, which was that he had been walking since noon at the pace of a man who expects to arrive somewhere.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Thirty-One</h3>
<p class="p">The country went on being not quite the Waste for a long time before it was anything else.</p>
<p class="p">Nobody had told him about that, and there was no day on which anything had improved enough to name. Water more often. Not water. Ground that took a print, so that he could see where he had walked, which he had not been able to do for two years and which he found he did not enjoy as much as he had expected.</p>
<p class="p">He passed a thing on the tenth day out from the watercourse that he did not understand.</p>
<p class="p">It was in a wide shallow bowl of ground below him, and there were hundreds of them — carts, wagons, some with the shafts still up and some with the wheels off and stacked — and the whole floor of the bowl was covered in crates and boxes and bundles set out in rows, hundreds of rows, with lanes between them. There were people. They were moving between the rows with things in their arms, and stopping, and setting things down, and picking other things up. Some of them were arguing. He could hear it as a sound rather than as words, the particular rise and fall of people who have been having the same argument for a long time.</p>
<p class="p">It had been there a while. There were paths worn between the wagons and a smell of smoke from more than one fire and washing strung on a line between two of the larger carts.</p>
<p class="p">He watched for most of an hour.</p>
<p class="p">Nobody came up. Once a woman near the edge looked up in his direction and then went back to what she was doing, the way you do when a shape on a ridge is not your problem.</p>
<p class="p">He did not go down. He could not have said why, except that he had no idea what they were doing and did not want to be told badly, and there was some part of him that had learned in a garden not to walk into somebody else's arrangement and start improving it.</p>
<p class="p">He went on north-west along the rim and lost sight of the bowl by evening.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Thirty-Two</h3>
<p class="p">He was ill in the spring.</p>
<p class="p">It was nothing dramatic. Bad water, probably, from a pool he had known was doubtful and had drunk anyway because the seam before it had been dry. He was two days on his side under an overhang with his pack for a pillow, and it went through him, and there was one night in the middle of it where he was cold in a way that frightened him.</p>
<p class="p">Nobody came. That was the fact of it. He lay under a rock in a country with a name he did not know and was ill and nobody came, and there was no smoke on any horizon, and he understood — not as an idea, lying there, but in his body — what it had meant that there had been a fire in the Waste for eleven years.</p>
<p class="p">He thought: she is right. She should not come.</p>
<p class="p">Then, in the same hour, because he was ill and honest: I would like her to come.</p>
<p class="p">Both of those stayed with him after he got up.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">He came out of it thin and slow and started walking again, and about a week later he did a thing he did not plan.</p>
<p class="p">He was up on high ground in the late afternoon and there was dead scrub in a hollow, quite a lot of it, dry as paper. He had gathered wood without thinking about it — it was a habit from a place where there was none — and it was in his hand and there was a hollow full of tinder and he had flint.</p>
<p class="p">He built a fire.</p>
<p class="p">It was not cold enough to need one. He built it anyway, and got it going, and then did the thing that made it something other than a fire: he built it up too big, and put green stuff on it, and stood back and watched the smoke go up in a column against a flat sky where anybody within a day's walk would see it.</p>
<p class="p">Then he sat down beside it and waited to see if anyone came.</p>
<p class="p">Nobody came. He kept it up until the middle of the night and slept beside the embers and woke at first light and there was nobody, and he put it out properly, scattering it and treading it, and stood there in the burnt patch feeling foolish in a way he could not get rid of all morning.</p>
<p class="p">He did the same thing eleven days later, in a different place, and again nobody came, and he was less foolish about it the second time.</p>
<p class="p">The third time somebody came.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Thirty-Three</h3>
<p class="p">He came over the rise at a trot, which was wrong.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast had built the fire at midday and had been sitting beside it since, feeding it green stuff, watching the column stand up in the still air, and he had got as far as thinking about how many more of these he could afford in wood before the country ran out of hollows. Then there was a man on the skyline coming fast and downhill with his pack bouncing, and Steadfast stood up.</p>
<p class="p">He was young. Twenty-five, perhaps. He arrived out of breath and put his hands on his knees and got the words out between pulls.</p>
<p class="d">— Are you all right?</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast looked at him.</p>
<p class="d">— What?</p>
<p class="d">— The fire. Is it you? Are you hurt? Is there somebody down?</p>
<p class="p">And Steadfast stood in front of his own signal with his mouth open and understood what he had built. Not an invitation. A distress column. In a country where a man lights a fire that size in daylight for one reason, he had been lighting them for weeks and had thought of himself as a hearth.</p>
<p class="d">— No, he said. — Nobody is hurt.</p>
<p class="p">The young man straightened up. He looked at the fire, and at the green stuff stacked ready beside it, and then at Steadfast, and something went across his face that was not quite suspicion but was a long way from what Steadfast had been expecting.</p>
<p class="d">— Right, he said.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">His name was Constant, which he gave without being asked, in the manner of a man who likes his name.</p>
<p class="p">He had water. That was the first thing Steadfast noticed and could not stop noticing — two skins, both full, and he offered Steadfast a drink from one before he had been offered anything, and Steadfast said no thank you and then wanted it for the rest of the evening.</p>
<p class="p">He had food. He had a good pack, well made, and a coat that had been mended by somebody who could mend, and he had come nine days from a place he named as though Steadfast would know it, and when Steadfast did not know it he described it cheerfully and at length.</p>
<p class="p">He talked. That was the other thing. He talked the whole time he was setting his things down and the whole time the pot was on and most of the time he was eating, and none of it was empty — it was information, it was where the water was on the north side of the pale stone and which of the hollows had wood in them and what the weather did here in the autumn — and Steadfast, who had spent two years learning to sit inside a silence until the other person filled it, found that this one would not stop being filled.</p>
<p class="p">He tried Grieve's methods anyway, because they were what he had.</p>
<p class="p">He fed him first and asked him nothing, and Constant filled the silence with an account of the pale stone that went on for a quarter of an hour, and thanked him for the food, and did not seem to notice that he had not been asked a single question.</p>
<p class="p">He waited. Constant waited too, comfortably, and then started talking again.</p>
<p class="p">At one point Steadfast said nothing for long enough that it became a thing in the air, and Constant looked at him and said: — Are you all right? You keep doing that.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Thirty-Four</h3>
<p class="p">In the morning Constant showed him the map.</p>
<p class="p">He got it out over the last of the fire, unrolled it on a flat stone with two pebbles to hold the corners, and it was beautiful. That was the trouble with it. It was inked in three colours on good hide, with the folds marked, and the seams marked, and a scale, and a compass rose in the corner drawn by somebody who could draw.</p>
<p class="d">— There, said Constant, with his finger on it. — Thirteen days from here, fourteen if the ground is soft.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast looked where the finger was.</p>
<p class="p">It was the road.</p>
<p class="p">It was drawn as a double line running north and south across the top third of the map, confident, unbroken, with the lodging-houses marked along it as little squares, and there was one square with a name he knew because he had slept in it for nineteen years' worth of nights.</p>
<p class="d">— You are going to the road, Steadfast said.</p>
<p class="d">— I am going back to it. I came off it four years ago in the bad autumn, same as everybody, and I have been eighteen months getting a straight answer out of anyone about where it runs now, and this — he tapped the hide — this is from a man who had it surveyed.</p>
<p class="d">— Surveyed.</p>
<p class="d">— He has people out. He sells them at a fair price, and before you say it, I know what you are going to say, I have heard it — he did not sound impatient; he sounded like a man who had won this argument before and was fond of winning it — everyone says the road is gone. Everyone has always said that. The people who say it are the people who stopped looking. He did not stop looking. He went and put a line on paper.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast looked at the beautiful map for a while.</p>
<p class="d">— I walked that road nineteen years, he said.</p>
<p class="p">Constant's face came up, and it was the worst thing that had happened to Steadfast in a year, because what was in it was delight.</p>
<p class="d">— Then you know it, he said. — Then you can tell me — is that the run north of the ford, there, does it bend like that, because he has it bending and I have had two people tell me it runs straight —</p>
<p class="p">And Steadfast opened his mouth to say: it does not bend and it does not run straight, because it is not there, I stood in the doorway of that square you have marked and there was grass where the road had been and it was old grass, and no line drawn by any surveyor has been within a hundred miles of the truth.</p>
<p class="p">And he did not say it.</p>
<p class="p">He heard himself not saying it. He stood over a young man on his knees at a map and did the thing he had watched done: he let the silence be where the answer went, and told himself, in the exact words, that the boy had not asked him whether the road was there. He had asked about a bend.</p>
<p class="d">— It bends, Steadfast said.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Thirty-Five</h3>
<p class="p">It was true. It had bent north of the ford. It had bent that way for as long as anybody had walked it, and the surveyor had got that one thing right, and Constant was pleased and rolled the map up and put it away and slept well.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast did not sleep.</p>
<p class="p">He went over it in the dark and found that he could construct it perfectly. Grieve would not have told him. Grieve had stood in her own yard and let a girl walk east into nothing with a bundle and a flask because the girl had not asked her what was out there, and when Steadfast had screamed at her about it she had not defended it as a principle, she had said she had chosen which not-knowing she could carry.</p>
<p class="p">So he had done what she did.</p>
<p class="p">Except that Grieve had known what was east — she had known it as an absence, a country with nothing in it — and she had told Ash the moment Ash asked. She had answered the question that was put to her, fully, in about a minute, and had not put a hand out to stop her.</p>
<p class="p">He had been asked a question and had answered a different one.</p>
<p class="p">He lay in the dark and got no further with it than that, and it was enough.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">In the morning he tried.</p>
<p class="d">— The map, he said, while Constant was strapping his pack. — The man who made it. Did you meet him.</p>
<p class="d">— I did. Good man. Not what you would expect, either — no fuss about him, he did not oversell it, he said outright there were parts he was not sure of and he marked them lighter, look — and he had the map out again, and there they were, three stretches inked in a paler hand, honest as anything.</p>
<p class="d">— And the road itself. Did he say he had seen it.</p>
<p class="p">Constant paused, and for a moment Steadfast thought it had gone in.</p>
<p class="d">— He said his people had.</p>
<p class="d">— Not him.</p>
<p class="d">— His people. That is what surveyors are, he said, and laughed, and went back to his straps.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast tried once more, and made a mess of it.</p>
<p class="d">— When you get there. If it is not — if the country has —</p>
<p class="d">— If it is not there, said Constant, standing up, and he said it kindly, which was the unbearable part. — I know. I do know. Everybody I have met in eighteen months has managed to say some version of that to me, usually about four sentences in, and you have lasted a whole night, which I am grateful for. If it is not there I will have walked thirteen days and I will know, and I would rather know than sit in a hollow with a fire wondering.</p>
<p class="p">He put his hand out. Steadfast shook it.</p>
<p class="d">— Thank you for the food, Constant said. — And for the bend.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Thirty-Six</h3>
<p class="p">He watched him go up the rise and over.</p>
<p class="p">He stood by the dead fire and watched him go, and told himself the things: that the boy had water, that the boy had food, that the boy had a better pack than his own and knew the hollows on the north side; that a man of twenty-five who has been eighteen months getting an answer is entitled to go and get one; that he had not lied about the bend; that Grieve would have let him go.</p>
<p class="p">That last one held for about an hour.</p>
<p class="p">Then it stopped holding, because he could hear how it worked — <em>Grieve would have let him go</em> — and it was not the same shape as anything Grieve had ever said. She had never once told him what to do by telling him what she would do. She had told him to read the person in front of him and then had refused, at cost, to be the reason for anything.</p>
<p class="p">And the person in front of him had not been half dead and had not needed feeding and had not needed to be left alone in a silence to arrive at his own conclusion. He had needed one sentence from the only man in the country who had stood in that doorway, and he had gone off carrying a beautiful map and a bend and a piece of plain courtesy, and Steadfast had given him the courtesy because it cost the least.</p>
<p class="p">He got his pack. He did not decide to, particularly. He rolled the blanket and kicked the burnt patch flat out of habit and was up on the rise inside a quarter of an hour, and then he was running, badly, which at his age and after a winter of bad water was a thing to see, and he lost the trail twice and found it because the ground here took a print, and he was two hours catching him.</p>
<p class="p">Constant heard him coming and turned round, and his hand went to his pack strap, and the look on his face was pure alarm — a big man labouring up behind you in empty country is not good news whoever he turns out to be — and Steadfast had to stop and get his breath before he could do anything about it.</p>
<p class="d">— It is gone, he said.</p>
<p class="p">Constant stared at him.</p>
<p class="d">— What?</p>
<p class="d">— The road. It is not there. I did not walk off it four years ago, I came out of the lodging one morning and it was gone, and there was grass where it had been and the grass was old. That square on your map with the name on it. I slept in that house nineteen years. I stood in that doorway and there was nothing.</p>
<p class="p">Constant did not say anything.</p>
<p class="d">— It bends north of the ford, Steadfast said. — That part is true and I told you the true part because it was the part that cost me nothing, and I have been up all night about it, and you can do what you like with the thirteen days but you are not going to spend them not knowing what I know.</p>
<p class="p">He ran out. There had not been much of it and he had used it all going up the hill.</p>
<p class="p">Constant looked at him for a long moment. Then he took his pack off, slowly, and sat down on it in the middle of the open ground, and put his hands over his face, and stayed like that.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast stood six feet away.</p>
<p class="p">He stood there long enough to feel it — the distance, the rightness of it, the whole clean architecture of leaving a man to arrive at his own grief without being crowded — and then he crossed it and sat down on the ground next to him, close enough that their shoulders were nearly touching, which was not something he had ever seen anybody do.</p>
<p class="p">Neither of them said anything for a long time.</p>
<p class="d">— Four years, Constant said, into his hands.</p>
<p class="d">— I know.</p>
<p class="d">— No, but four <em>years</em>.</p>
<p class="d">— I know, Steadfast said.</p>
<p class="p">And that was all he had, and it turned out to be the thing that was wanted, and they sat there on the open ground in the middle of the morning with the map still rolled up in the pack under him.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Thirty-Seven</h3>
<p class="p">Constant was not quick about it and Steadfast did not try to make him be.</p>
<p class="p">They stayed on that open ground the whole of the day. Around noon Constant got up and walked off about two hundred yards and stood with his back to everything for a while, and came back, and sat down again, and Steadfast made tea because it was the only thing he could think of to do with his hands.</p>
<p class="p">In the middle of the afternoon Constant said: — Four years is not the worst of it.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— Eighteen months is the worst of it. Four years I was just lost, everybody was lost. Eighteen months I have been telling people. I have been telling them confidently.</p>
<p class="p">He laughed, once, badly.</p>
<p class="d">— I told a woman at a well in the spring that the people who say it is gone are the people who stopped looking. I said it to her face. She had a dead husband and she had stopped looking and I said that to her.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast did not say <em>you were not to know</em>, which was in his mouth and would have been for himself.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes, he said instead.</p>
<p class="p">Constant looked at him.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not much comfort.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— Good, said Constant, and put his head back in his hands, and stayed there until the light started to go.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">In the evening he took the map out.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast watched him do it and had a moment of real fear, because he did not know which way it would go and there was a version of the next ten minutes that ended with a beautiful piece of hide in the fire and a man feeling clean for about a day and hollow for a year.</p>
<p class="p">Constant unrolled it. He looked at it for a long time. Then he put his finger on one of the pale-inked stretches, the honest ones, the parts the surveyor had admitted he was not sure of.</p>
<p class="d">— He did do that, he said. — He did do that much.</p>
<p class="p">He rolled it up and put it away.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast let out a breath he had not known he was holding, and understood he had almost said something about it, and was glad he had not, and did not know which of those three was the useful one.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Thirty-Eight</h3>
<p class="p">They went on together without either of them proposing it.</p>
<p class="p">There was a morning where they were both packed and standing, and Constant said</p>
<p class="d">— which way do you go, and Steadfast said north-west, meaning nothing by it except that it was the way he had been going, and Constant said all right, and that was the arrangement for the next four months.</p>
<p class="p">It was nothing like the garden.</p>
<p class="p">Constant talked. He never stopped talking, and Steadfast learned that he had been wrong about what that was — it was not confidence, it was how the man kept himself company, and he had been doing it alone for eighteen months. He talked about weather and about his mother and about a dog he had owned and about the correct way to strap a pack, which he taught Steadfast, and which was better than Steadfast's way and had been for four months before Steadfast admitted it.</p>
<p class="p">He was better in country than Steadfast was. That was the other thing.</p>
<p class="p">He read cloud. He found water twice where Steadfast had walked past it, both times without any triumph about it, once by watching where a bird went. He knew plants. He had a way of walking uphill, a short-stepped patient thing, that Steadfast copied badly and then well.</p>
<p class="p">They divided the work without ever discussing it, and after the first month Steadfast noticed that most of the practical decisions were being made by the younger man and that he did not mind, and then noticed that he had spent nineteen years being the person who made them.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">He did try to give Constant something and it went wrong twice before it went right.</p>
<p class="p">The first time he tried to talk about the road, in the evening, in a way he had rehearsed. He had a whole shape for it about how the country had turned rather than the road moving, which was the keeper's shape, and he got about four sentences in before he heard what he sounded like.</p>
<p class="p">Constant heard it too and was kind about it.</p>
<p class="d">— That is somebody else's, is it not.</p>
<p class="d">— A man said it to me. The morning it went.</p>
<p class="d">— It sounds like it, Constant said, not unkindly, and let it go.</p>
<p class="p">The second time was worse and involved advice about grief which Steadfast will not be quoted on.</p>
<p class="p">The third time he did not try at all.</p>
<p class="p">They had stopped early because of a hard rain and were sitting under an overhang watching it come down, and Constant was restless in the way he got when he was not walking, and there was a scatter of flat stones at the mouth of the shelter where water had cut the bank. Steadfast picked one up, out of habit, and turned it over, and did not lift the next one, and turned that one where it lay instead.</p>
<p class="p">Constant watched him for a minute.</p>
<p class="d">— What are you doing.</p>
<p class="d">— Nothing. Looking at the face.</p>
<p class="d">— The face.</p>
<p class="p">So Steadfast showed him.</p>
<p class="p">It took the rest of the afternoon and it was the first thing he had been any good at teaching. You do not pick it up. You turn it where it lies and look at the face it gives you, and if the face is wrong you leave it and try another, and the whole art of it is in not lifting anything until you are sure, because lifting is the expensive part.</p>
<p class="p">Constant was terrible at it. He lifted everything. He forced two stones together that did not want to go and made a small bad wall against the bank with a bulge in the middle of it, and Steadfast opened his mouth to tell him to take it out and then did not.</p>
<p class="d">— That will come down, he said instead.</p>
<p class="d">— I can fix it.</p>
<p class="d">— Leave it. It will come down or it will not, and either way you will learn more from watching it than from me telling you.</p>
<p class="p">He heard the words come out of his own mouth in the order she had put them and had to turn away and look at the rain for a while.</p>
<p class="p">It did not come down. It was still standing when they left two days later, and Constant looked back at it from the top of the bank with an expression Steadfast knew from the inside.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Thirty-Nine</h3>
<p class="p">Constant asked his name in the second month.</p>
<p class="p">That was itself a thing — that it took two months — and it came out sideways, at a crossing, while they were arguing about whether to ford or go up.</p>
<p class="d">— You have not told me your name, Constant said. — I have been calling you <em>you</em> for eight weeks and I have got away with it because there is nobody else here, but if we meet anybody I am going to look like a man who does not know who he is travelling with.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast was in the water at the time, feeling for the bottom with one boot.</p>
<p class="p">He opened his mouth.</p>
<p class="p">Nothing came out of it.</p>
<p class="p">He had said the word perhaps four hundred times in two years, at a table, in a yard, every day, in the ordinary way, and he had flinched every time and had never once failed to say it. And now, standing in a river in a country with no name in front of a young man who had asked a reasonable question, he could not get it out of his mouth at all.</p>
<p class="p">Constant waited. Then, because he was not stupid, he stopped waiting.</p>
<p class="d">— All right, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— It is not —</p>
<p class="d">— No, said Constant. — Really. It is all right. You have not asked me a single thing about the well or the woman since the day you told me, and I have noticed that, and I am not going to start charging you for the food.</p>
<p class="p">He came down the bank into the water.</p>
<p class="d">— Up, he said. — Not across. Look at the colour of it on the far side.</p>
<p class="p">They went up.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">He was awake most of that night and could not work out what had happened to him, because the name had not become a lie in the river. It had been a lie for two years and he had said it fine.</p>
<p class="p">What had changed was that there was somebody in front of him who would have taken it seriously.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve had known. Grieve had asked <em>is that yours</em>, on the first evening, and had got the answer and had never mentioned it again, and had gone on using it every day in the ordinary way because she had understood that a man who cannot say his own name yet still has to be called something. She had been carrying it for him the whole time. Two years of <em>pass me that, Steadfast</em> had not been her failing to notice. It had been her holding the thing steady until he could pick it up.</p>
<p class="p">He lay in the dark with that and it was the first time since he had walked out of her gap in the wall that he had missed her without wanting anything from her.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Forty</h3>
<p class="p">The country changed for good in the fourth month and neither of them made anything of it at the time.</p>
<p class="p">They came down a long shoulder of ground in the afternoon and there was a watercourse at the bottom, and there was water in it.</p>
<p class="p">Not a seam. Not a cup at the lip of a rise after twenty minutes of patience. Water moving, a hand deep and two paces across, going somewhere with a noise.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast stopped on the slope and Constant went past him and down and was in it to the knees before Steadfast had moved, splashing about like an idiot, shouting something.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast came down slowly and crouched at the edge and put his hand in and let the water go over it.</p>
<p class="p">He filled a flask. He held it up against the light to read the level, at the angle, with the tilt of the head, and then stopped, and looked at what he was doing, and laughed at himself out loud for the first time in a very long time — because there was a river in front of him, and he was rationing.</p>
<p class="p">He tipped it out and filled it again and drank the whole thing standing up.</p>
<p class="p">They followed the water for twelve days. It got bigger. On the sixth day there were trees, four of them, small and wind-bent and entirely miraculous, and Constant climbed one for no reason and could not get down as easily as he had got up, and Steadfast stood underneath and gave him instructions that were no help at all.</p>
<p class="p">On the ninth day they saw the smoke of somebody else's fire in the distance, and it was not a signal, and neither of them went to it.</p>
<p class="p">On the twelfth day the watercourse turned south and they had to decide whether to follow it.</p>
<p class="d">— It goes somewhere, Constant said.</p>
<p class="d">— It goes south.</p>
<p class="d">— Things go where water goes. That is generally the whole of it.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast looked south along the run of it, at the green either side, at the way the ground fell away easy for as far as he could see.</p>
<p class="p">Then he looked north-west, at nothing in particular. Rising ground. No water that he could see. The same grey-green scrub they had been walking through for four months, going up.</p>
<p class="d">— I want to go up, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— Why.</p>
<p class="p">And Steadfast stood there and found, with some surprise, that he had an answer, and that it was not about the road.</p>
<p class="d">— Because I have been going that way since I came over the pale stone, he said,</p>
<p class="d">— and I have not decided anything since. I would like to decide something.</p>
<p class="p">Constant looked at the easy green south, and then at the hard grey up.</p>
<p class="d">— That is a stupid reason, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes.</p>
<p class="d">— I am not saying it is a bad one.</p>
<p class="p">They went up.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Forty-One</h3>
<p class="p">The hard grey up went on for six weeks and was worth nothing, and then it was worth everything, which is how ground works.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast nearly killed himself on it in the fourth week. There was a slope of loose shale that had a good line across it and a better line above, and he took the good one because it was quicker, and was forty feet out on it when it started to go under him in a slow soft way that he had never felt before and did not understand.</p>
<p class="p">He heard Constant say his name — say <em>you</em>, rather, in the voice you use — and then he was on his face in the scree with a hand round his forearm and both of them sliding, and then not sliding, and then lying there breathing.</p>
<p class="d">— The wet line, said Constant, when he could. — You cannot see it but you can hear it. It sounds different underfoot. Duller.</p>
<p class="d">— I did not hear anything.</p>
<p class="d">— No, said Constant. — You were going quickly.</p>
<p class="p">It was the first time in two and a half years Steadfast had been in real danger and it had come from ground, which he had thought he understood, and it had been read correctly by a man twenty-five years younger who had never crossed a waste in his life.</p>
<p class="p">He was quiet for the rest of that day. Constant let him be, and did not mention it again, which was its own kind of mercy and which Steadfast recognised, because it was one of his.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">The habits went at different speeds. He kept a list of that too, without meaning to.</p>
<p class="p">The rationing went first, in the river, laughing.</p>
<p class="p">The wood-gathering did not go at all — he still picked up anything dry that he passed, and by then they were in country with wood in it and Constant had stopped teasing him about the bundle and had started using it.</p>
<p class="p">The waiting stayed. He still let a silence run three beats past comfortable before he filled it, and it still made Constant look at him, and he was not going to stop.</p>
<p class="p">And the smoke went. He had not built a column since the day Constant came over the rise at a trot. He told himself it was because he had company now, and knew that was not the whole of it, and did not go further with it than that.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Forty-Two</h3>
<p class="p">They came over the top in the seventh week and there was a valley, and there was smoke in it — four or five threads of it, low, domestic, going up straight — and Constant stopped and looked at Steadfast.</p>
<p class="d">— We are going down, he said. It was not a question. — I want to be somewhere with people in it and I have wanted it for a month and I have not said so because you seem to like this.</p>
<p class="d">— I do not like this.</p>
<p class="d">— You like it more than I do.</p>
<p class="p">They went down.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">There were perhaps forty of them, and the first thing was that nobody made anything of the two of them arriving.</p>
<p class="p">A woman with a child on her hip pointed them at a long low building where there was food and told them to help themselves and went back to what she was doing. A man mending a hinge looked up and said good evening. Somebody's dog came and smelled Steadfast's leg thoroughly and left.</p>
<p class="p">Constant found this delightful and had a conversation with four separate people before the light went. Steadfast found it enormously hard, in a way he had not been prepared for, and had to go and stand at the edge of the buildings for a while and look at the dark and be alone with his heart going, and then come back in.</p>
<p class="p">Nobody remarked on that either.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">He saw the road in the morning.</p>
<p class="p">He had gone out early because he always did and there was a track running out of the north end of the valley, and he was on it before he understood what he was looking at.</p>
<p class="p">It was made. That was the thing. Not worn — <em>made</em>. There was a bed of broken stone under it and a camber to it so the water would come off, and a lip of set kerb along the downhill side where the ground fell away, and it had been laid by hand, badly in places and well in others, in sections that did not quite match because different people had done them.</p>
<p class="p">He walked out along it. It went for about two hours and then stopped.</p>
<p class="p">It stopped at a well. That was all. A well, with a wall round it and a windlass and a stone trough, and the road ran to it and ended there and did not go anywhere else.</p>
<p class="p">He walked back the other way and it ran down the valley and past the buildings and out the far end and stopped again, at the edge of a wide flat of good ground with half of it under crops and half of it still to break.</p>
<p class="p">Twelve miles, perhaps. Well to field. That was the whole of it.</p>
<p class="p">He stood in the middle of it for a long time.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Forty-Three</h3>
<p class="p">Constant hated it.</p>
<p class="p">That was not obvious at first because Constant hated it politely, and made conversation, and asked good questions of the man who was explaining it — how long, who started it, where the stone came from — and it took Steadfast most of the morning to hear what was underneath the questions.</p>
<p class="p">They had been at it nine years. It had started because two families on the flat could not get water up in the wet months when the ground went to mud, and someone had put down a raft of broken stone across the worst forty yards, and that had worked, and then they had done the next forty because they were already dirty.</p>
<p class="d">— And it will go further, said the man, cheerfully. — There is talk of taking it south to the crossing. That is another six miles. That is probably me dead before that is done but somebody will do it.</p>
<p class="d">— Right, said Constant.</p>
<p class="p">That night he did not sleep either.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">He said it at the edge of the buildings with his back against a wall.</p>
<p class="d">— It is obscene.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast waited.</p>
<p class="d">— It is twelve miles. It goes from a hole in the ground to a field. You cannot get anywhere on it. A man could walk the whole of it and back before his dinner and be exactly where he started, and they have been building it for nine years, and they talk about it —</p>
<p class="p">He stopped and started again.</p>
<p class="d">— They talk about it the way you talk about a road.</p>
<p class="d">— It is a road.</p>
<p class="d">— It is not a road, Constant said, and his voice went, and he got it back. — A road goes somewhere you have not been. That is the whole of what a road is. That is the only thing a road is for. This is a — this is a very long yard.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast let the silence run. Three beats. Four.</p>
<p class="d">— Say the rest, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— There is no rest.</p>
<p class="d">— There is.</p>
<p class="p">Constant put his head back against the wall and looked up at nothing for a while.</p>
<p class="d">— If that is a road, he said, — then the people who built it are people who stopped looking. And they are happy. They are the happiest people I have met in four years and they stopped looking nine years ago and made a yard and called it a road, and if that is allowed — if that is <em>allowed</em> —</p>
<p class="p">And there it was.</p>
<p class="d">— Then you were right about them, Steadfast said.</p>
<p class="d">— Then I was right about them and wrong about everything else, said Constant. — Then that woman at the well had stopped looking and I told her so and she went home and I was correct.</p>
<p class="p">He was crying by then, in the way men do who have decided not to, which is worse to watch.</p>
<p class="d">— I would rather have been a liar, he said. — Do you understand that? For eighteen months I have wanted somebody to prove me wrong about the road because being wrong about the road makes me only a fool. This makes me a fool who was <em>right about her</em> and said it out loud to her face in front of a well.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast did not have anything.</p>
<p class="p">That was the honest position and he stayed in it for a while, which was harder than talking. He thought about saying that she had probably forgotten. He thought about saying that being right and being cruel are different failures and Constant had only committed one of them. Both of those were true and both of them were for Steadfast's comfort and he did not say either.</p>
<p class="p">What he said in the end was:</p>
<p class="d">— I do not think that road is people who stopped looking.</p>
<p class="d">— Then you have not looked at it.</p>
<p class="d">— I have walked it twice.</p>
<p class="d">— Then what is it.</p>
<p class="p">And Steadfast, who had spent two and a half years learning not to hand people finished things, found that he wanted very badly to hand this one over, and could not make it into anything.</p>
<p class="d">— It is somebody's forty yards, he said. — And then somebody else's next forty. That is all I have got. I stood on it this morning and I could see where the sections change hands and I have not been able to stop thinking about it and I cannot tell you why.</p>
<p class="p">Constant wiped his face with the heel of his hand.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not an argument.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— You are supposed to be the one who knows things.</p>
<p class="d">— I have never once been that, said Steadfast, and it came out with more in it than he had intended, and Constant looked at him properly for the first time that evening.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Forty-Four</h3>
<p class="p">They stayed twelve days, and by the end of them Constant had helped lay about sixty feet of the southern extension, which Steadfast did not remark on and thought about constantly.</p>
<p class="p">He was good at it. Of course he was. He was good at everything in country and he had a back on him and he learned the camber in an afternoon from a woman who had been doing it for six years, and he worked hard and laughed with them and came in filthy, and at night he lay on his back and did not talk.</p>
<p class="p">On the thirteenth morning he was packed.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast found him at the end of the buildings with his pack on and knew before anything was said, the way you do.</p>
<p class="d">— I am going back to the well, Constant said.</p>
<p class="d">— Which well.</p>
<p class="d">— Hers.</p>
<p class="p">He said it flatly, as a fact he had been carrying around for twelve days and had finished carrying.</p>
<p class="d">— It is four months if I go hard. Probably five. I do not know if she is still there and I do not know what I would say and I have gone round it eleven days and I cannot get past it, so I am going to go and find out and stop constructing it.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast said: — Yes.</p>
<p class="p">Not <em>are you sure</em>. Not <em>she may have moved on</em>. Not <em>you could stay until the spring and go with better weather</em>, which was true, and which he wanted to say so badly that his jaw ached.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes, he said. — Go.</p>
<p class="p">Constant nodded. He looked out at the valley for a moment, and then at the track running down it.</p>
<p class="d">— I still think it is a yard.</p>
<p class="d">— I know.</p>
<p class="d">— But I laid sixty feet of it, said Constant, — and I would like it noted that I laid it properly, and if it is a yard then it is a very well-made yard and the camber is correct.</p>
<p class="d">— It is noted.</p>
<p class="p">They stood there.</p>
<p class="d">— Listen, said Constant. — I am going to say this badly. You have not told me your name and I have not asked twice, and I am not asking now. But somebody is going to ask you and you are going to have to say something, and when you do I would not say the old one.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— That is all. That is the whole of my advice and it is not much and you can see why I mostly talk about packs.</p>
<p class="p">He put his hand out. Steadfast took it, and then did not let go of it, and pulled him in and held on to him for a moment, which was not something either of them had done, and Constant made a sound and hung on.</p>
<p class="p">Then he went.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast watched him up the north track, on the made road, past the place where the sections changed hands, out to where it stopped at the well — and then off the end of it, onto the open ground, going east.</p>
<p class="p">He watched until he could not see him.</p>
<p class="p">Then he went back down into the valley, and somebody put a shovel in his hands before he had said anything, and he took it.</p>
<div class="partpage"><h1>PART FOUR</h1><h2>The Table Lands</h2></div>
<h3 class="sec">Forty-Five</h3>
<p class="p">Nobody asked him to stay and nobody asked him to go, and that was how he came to be there seven years later.</p>
<p class="p">The shovel was the whole of it. Somebody put it in his hands on the morning Constant left and he went down to the southern end and worked until the light went, and in the evening he ate in the long building with everyone else, and in the morning somebody put a shovel in his hands again. That was the arrangement. Nobody wrote it down. He asked once, in the first month, who he should speak to about whether he could stay through the winter, and the man he asked — Ivo, the one who had been mending a hinge on the first evening, and who turned out to mend most things — looked genuinely puzzled and said: — Speak to about what?</p>
<p class="p">It took Steadfast most of a season to stop expecting to be assessed.</p>
<p class="p">That was harder than the work. He had spent nineteen years in a world where you were placed — you were on the road or you were not, you were at a stage or you were behind it, and there was always somebody whose business it was to know which. Here there was nothing to be at. There was a road that needed four hundred yards extending before the wet, and there were people doing it, and if you did it you were doing it.</p>
<p class="p">He was bad at this for a long time. In the second month he worked nineteen days without stopping because some part of him believed he was still earning the food, and on the nineteenth day Rell came down the line, looked at what he had laid, and said:</p>
<p class="d">— This is worse than yesterday.</p>
<p class="d">— I have done twice as much as —</p>
<p class="d">— I know what you have done. It is worse. Go and sit down.</p>
<p class="p">He did not go and sit down. He worked another two hours and made it worse still, and then went and sat down, and did not sleep well, and came back in the morning and took out most of the previous day.</p>
<p class="p">Rell did not mention it. She had been laying road for nine years and had run out of interest in men who could not be told things.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Forty-Six</h3>
<p class="p">There were forty-one of them, and by the end of the first winter he could have told you most of what mattered about a dozen.</p>
<p class="p"><strong>Rell</strong> ran the road, in the sense that nobody else did. She was perhaps fifty and had come into the valley from the north as a girl with her mother, and had been on the second forty yards, and had never in her life been on the King's Road or wanted to be. She was not warm. She was exact and she was fair, and when she told you a thing was wrong she told you once, and she never once told anybody the same thing twice, which meant everybody listened the first time.</p>
<p class="p"><strong>Ivo</strong> was older and mended. Hinges, wheels, the windlass at the well, a boy's arm once when it was set badly. He talked while he worked in a low continuous way that did not require answering, and Steadfast, who had spent two years with a woman who did not talk and a year with a man who did not stop, found that he could sit in Ivo's workshop for a whole afternoon and be perfectly comfortable and afterwards not be able to say what had been discussed.</p>
<p class="p"><strong>Sal</strong> was eight and belonged to everybody in the loose way children in small places do. She had two questions she asked adults, in rotation, and the first was whether they had ever seen the sea.</p>
<p class="p">And there was <strong>Petch</strong>, who was nineteen and had been born in the valley and had never been anywhere, and who was going to matter later.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">He got his own section in the spring.</p>
<p class="p">That was not a ceremony either. Rell walked the southern end with him and stopped and said: — From that stake to the elbow. That is yours. Do not ask me about it every day.</p>
<p class="p">It was ninety yards of the worst ground on the extension, wet in three places and crossed by a run-off that had to be got under the road rather than over it, and it took him a hundred and sixty days.</p>
<p class="p">He got the culvert wrong twice. The first time he laid it too shallow and had to take up forty feet of finished bed to redo it, in the rain, alone, because it was his section and everybody knew it was his section. The second time he got the fall right and the mouth wrong and it silted in the first wet month, and he dug it out and packed the mouth with set stone and it held.</p>
<p class="p">On the day he finished, nobody came. He walked up and down it twice and then went in for his dinner.</p>
<p class="p">Three days later Rell said, on her way past: — Your elbow is holding.</p>
<p class="p">That was all, and he thought about it for a week.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Forty-Seven</h3>
<p class="p">The winter after that, the road failed.</p>
<p class="p">Not his section. Four hundred yards north of the buildings, laid nine years earlier, in the second year, when they had been doing forty yards at a time with no camber and no bed under it because nobody had known any better yet. There was a hard freeze and then a fast thaw and the whole of it lifted and broke and went to mud, and a carter coming down with the winter grain went in to the axle and snapped it, and they lost most of a load in the wet.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast expected an inquest. That was the shape his mind still made: somebody had laid that badly, somebody would be answerable, there would be a reckoning of some kind and probably a meeting.</p>
<p class="p">What happened was that about thirty people went up there the next morning with tools.</p>
<p class="p">They took the whole four hundred yards out. They did it over nine days in the cold, in shifts, and the old woman who had laid the original section — she was seventy-something and did not work any more and came up to watch — stood at the edge of it the whole first day, and at one point Steadfast heard Rell say to her, without any edge at all:</p>
<p class="d">— We did not know about the bed.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— Nobody knew about the bed.</p>
<p class="d">— I knew it was wrong when the water stood on it, said the old woman. — I did not know why.</p>
<p class="p">And Rell said: — Well. Now we know why.</p>
<p class="p">And that was the entire reckoning, and they relaid it properly, and the old woman came up every day of the nine and no one suggested she should not.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast worked the last three days of it and thought about a bulge in a wall eight hundred miles away and did not know what to do with the thought, so he carried stone.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Forty-Eight</h3>
<p class="p">Sal asked him his name in the second summer, on the road, while he was eating his bread.</p>
<p class="p">She had asked him about the sea already, twice, and he had said no both times, which had disappointed her enough that she had moved on to her other question.</p>
<p class="d">— What are you called?</p>
<p class="p">He had known this was coming for two years. He had not prepared anything, because preparing something would have meant deciding, and he had not decided.</p>
<p class="d">— I have not got one just now, he said.</p>
<p class="p">Sal considered this with complete seriousness.</p>
<p class="d">— Everybody has got one.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes.</p>
<p class="d">— So what happened to yours.</p>
<p class="d">— I put it down.</p>
<p class="d">— Where?</p>
<p class="d">— A long way back.</p>
<p class="p">She thought about that, sitting on the kerb with her heels against the set stone.</p>
<p class="d">— Was it a bad one?</p>
<p class="p">And he found that he had to think about it, and that thinking about it in front of an eight-year-old was the first time he had looked at it straight.</p>
<p class="d">— No, he said. — It was a good one. It was just not true.</p>
<p class="d">— Oh, said Sal.</p>
<p class="p">She swung her heels for a bit.</p>
<p class="d">— Are you going to go and get it?</p>
<p class="p">He did not answer that, and she did not notice, because she had seen somebody with a dog further up the road and was gone.</p>
<p class="p">He sat on the kerb for a long time after that with the rest of his bread in his hand.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">She did not tell anybody, or she told everybody and nobody minded, which in that valley amounted to the same thing. What happened was that people went on calling him nothing at all. If they needed him they came and found him. If they were talking about him to somebody else, they said <em>the one on the elbow</em>, and after a while they said <em>the elbow</em>, and it turned out that a man could be a place.</p>
<p class="p">He noticed that Ivo never used it and never asked. Ivo said <em>you</em>, always, in a way that had no gap in it.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Forty-Nine</h3>
<p class="p">Petch was the one who made him angry, which nobody had done in three years.</p>
<p class="p">He was nineteen, born in the valley, and had never walked more than two days from it, and he had a way of talking about the road — the King's Road, the real one, the one nobody in the valley had ever used — that was not hostile exactly but was utterly without patience.</p>
<p class="p">It came out at the table one evening. Somebody had asked Steadfast something about the crossing at the ford, which meant explaining what the ford had been, which meant the road, and he was three sentences into it — carefully, without the keeper's shape, having learned that much — when Petch said:</p>
<p class="d">— Why does everyone from out there talk about it like a person who died.</p>
<p class="p">The table went a bit quiet.</p>
<p class="d">— Because it was a road, Petch said. — It was a good road, I have heard, it went a long way and people used it and now it does not. And every single one of you comes in here and sits down and cannot say four sentences without it. My mother came off it before I was born and she still —</p>
<p class="d">— That is enough, said Rell.</p>
<p class="d">— It is not, though, said Petch. — There is a road <em>outside</em>. It goes to the well. We built it. He built ninety yards of it and it took him half a year and the culvert is good, and I have never once heard anybody from out there call that a road without their voice doing a thing.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast, who had been about to say something conciliatory, discovered he was furious.</p>
<p class="d">— You have never lost one, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— Then you do not know.</p>
<p class="d">— No, said Petch, not backing off an inch. — I do not know. And you have never had one that was yours. Everything you have ever walked on was made by somebody else before you were born and you cannot even hear that you talk about it like an inheritance. I laid two hundred yards of that with my own hands when I was fifteen. You mourn the thing you were given. I am proud of the thing I made. Those are not the same and I am tired of being told mine is smaller.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">He was awake most of that night, which by then he knew the meaning of.</p>
<p class="p">The boy was wrong. That was the first thing and it stayed true: he was nineteen and had never had the ground go out from under him and there was a whole country of grief he had no idea existed, and he would have it one day and be sorry for some of what he had said.</p>
<p class="p">And he was right. That was the second thing, and it did not cancel the first, and Steadfast lay in the dark and made himself hold both, which was a thing he had been taught by a woman who held two kinds of not-knowing and had told him which one she could carry.</p>
<p class="p">He had walked the King's Road nineteen years and had never laid a stone of it. It had been there. It had been made by people whose names nobody had ever told him, and he had walked it and kept it and directed people onto it and had thought of that as service, and in three years in this valley he had put down ninety yards of bad ground and a culvert that took him two attempts, and he could not honestly say which of those was the larger thing.</p>
<p class="p">He apologised to Petch in the morning, badly, and Petch was mortified and said it was fine about eleven times, and after that the two of them worked well together for years.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Fifty</h3>
<p class="p">The valley ate together on the first evening of every month, and it took him until the third year to understand what that was.</p>
<p class="p">It was not a feast. The food was the same food. What was different was that everybody came — the whole forty-one, the babies, the old woman who had laid the bad section, the two men who did not speak to each other over something from before Steadfast arrived and who sat at opposite ends and both came anyway — and the tables went end to end down the middle of the long building so that it was one table.</p>
<p class="p">That was all. One table, once a month, everybody at it.</p>
<p class="p">The first year he sat at the end and could not eat much. The second year he found himself at the middle and did not notice until afterwards. The third year Rell put him on the bread, which meant walking the length of it with the boards and putting it down within reach of everybody, twice, and by the time he sat down his own food was cold and he did not care at all.</p>
<p class="p">He thought, that night, that he had spent two years in a garden with a woman who fed every stranger who came over the fold and had never once been to a table with more than three people at it.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Fifty-One</h3>
<p class="p">He started talking about the Waste in the fourth year, and it took him about six months to get it out properly.</p>
<p class="p">He did it badly at first. He tried, over a winter, to explain a woman in a garden to people who had never been thirsty, and heard himself making her into a story, and stopped for a month out of something like shame.</p>
<p class="p">Then he tried again with the practical parts, which turned out to be the way in. Eleven days of rain a year. A gutter of split wood repaired in four places with four woods. A second barrel for when you have got the first one wrong. Beans up canes in a country with no water and no soil worth the name, and a wall carried one stone at a time from somewhere you cannot see.</p>
<p class="p">Rell wanted to know about the wall. Ivo wanted to know about the gutter and asked four questions that Steadfast could not answer and was annoyed about not being able to answer, and wrote to nobody about it because there was nobody to write to, and instead drew it in the dirt three times over two evenings until he had something he liked better than Grieve's.</p>
<p class="p">And then one evening, at the end of it, he said the thing he had not planned to say.</p>
<p class="d">— She has been the only fire in that country for eleven years.</p>
<p class="p">They waited.</p>
<p class="d">— That is the whole of why she stays. There is nothing there that burns. If she goes, in a year there is no smoke over that fold, and the next one who comes out half dead walks past a mile east of it and never knows he walked past anything.</p>
<p class="p">He was looking at the table.</p>
<p class="d">— She counts the ones who found her. She has nine. And what she does instead of sleeping is think about the number she does not have.</p>
<p class="p">Nobody said anything for a while. Then Petch, who was twenty-three by then and had still never been anywhere, said:</p>
<p class="d">— How much would a fire cost.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Fifty-Two</h3>
<p class="p">It took two years and it was nothing like what Steadfast had imagined.</p>
<p class="p">What he had imagined, in the first hot week of it, was a chain — a line of fires across the Waste, kept, at intervals, so that nobody crossed more than four days without seeing smoke. He had it worked out on his second evening. He had the spacing.</p>
<p class="p">What actually happened was this.</p>
<p class="p">Ivo said the wood was the whole problem and spent a season on it and concluded that you could not carry enough and could not grow it there, and that anything burning in that country would have to burn something brought in, and that the carrying was the entire project and the fire was the easy part. That killed the chain. Four fires, at Steadfast's spacing, would need a wagon every six weeks across ground no wagon could cross.</p>
<p class="p">So it became one fire, at the eastern edge, where the ground was hard enough to bring a cart within two days of.</p>
<p class="p">Then it became one fire and a cache, because Rell said a fire with nobody at it was a fire for one night and a cache was a fire for a year, and they argued about that for a month, and in the end it was both — a stone hut with water and dry wood and food in a sealed pit, and a brazier on the roof of it that anybody staying there kept lit.</p>
<p class="p">Then it became the question of who stays, which nearly ended the whole thing, because the answer was that somebody had to live there and nobody in the valley wanted to live in the Waste, and Steadfast heard himself starting to say that he would go, and heard Grieve saying <em>if you stay, stay because you have found something here that is yours</em>, and stopped, and did not say it.</p>
<p class="p">In the end four households said they would take it in turn, a year each, and one of the two men who did not speak to the other put his hand up first, and that was the closest thing to a miracle Steadfast saw in the valley.</p>
<p class="p">It went out east in the sixth year. Two carts, nine people, forty days.</p>
<p class="p">He did not go with them.</p>
<p class="p">He wanted to. He had wanted to for two years and had assumed the whole time that he would, and when the day came he understood that if he went he would spend the entire forty days steering it, and that it had stopped being his about eighteen months earlier and everybody knew it except him.</p>
<p class="p">He stood on the road and watched the carts up the north end and out past the well, and Sal, who was thirteen by then, stood next to him and said: — Are you sad?</p>
<p class="d">— A bit.</p>
<p class="d">— Why?</p>
<p class="d">— Because I would have done it worse, he said, and she looked at him as though he had made a joke, and he had not, and he did not explain.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Fifty-Three</h3>
<p class="p">The carts came back in the spring with the hut built and the first household in it, and with a piece of news that Steadfast had not thought to want.</p>
<p class="p">There was already smoke out there.</p>
<p class="p">Not hers — hers was four days further west and they had not gone that far. This was another one, forty miles north of where they built, a man and two women who had put up something like a shelter at a seam and had been feeding people out of it for three years, and who had been astonished to see a cart, and who wanted to know whether more were coming.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast sat down on the step of the long building when he heard that, and had to put his hand over his mouth.</p>
<p class="p">Not because it was a triumph. Because for six years he had been carrying, without ever once looking at it directly, the belief that Grieve was alone out there in a way that nothing could be done about — that it was one woman and eleven years and a number she could not stop counting — and it had never occurred to him, not once, that somebody else might have had the same idea in the same country in the same years and neither of them would ever know.</p>
<p class="p">He thought about the man who saw the smoke and did not stop. He thought about Ash, and made himself stop thinking about Ash, which he had got better at and had never got good at.</p>
<p class="p">And then he thought: she does not know.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Fifty-Four</h3>
<p class="p">He told Rell first, in the workshop, which is where things got decided in that valley whatever anybody said about the table.</p>
<p class="d">— I am going to go back.</p>
<p class="p">Rell did not look up. — For how long.</p>
<p class="d">— I do not know.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not a plan.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="p">She turned the thing she was holding over.</p>
<p class="d">— Why.</p>
<p class="p">And Steadfast found that he had been getting ready to answer that for two years without knowing it, and that the answer had changed about four times, and that what was left was short.</p>
<p class="d">— She thinks she is the only one, he said. — She has thought it for twenty years. She is not, and there is a hut with a fire on it four days east of her, and there are three people forty miles north of that, and I know all of it and she knows none of it, and I am the only person alive who has been in both places.</p>
<p class="p">Rell put the thing down.</p>
<p class="d">— That is a good reason, she said. — I have not heard one of those in a while.</p>
<p class="d">— I am not going to bring her back.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— She will not come. I asked her once and I am not asking again.</p>
<p class="d">— Good, said Rell. — Then you are going for the right thing, and if you were going for the other one I would have said so.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">He was six weeks getting ready and most of it was other people's doing.</p>
<p class="p">Ivo made him a flask, unasked, of a design he had been improving for four years, and was rude about the old one. Petch, who had grown into a large calm man and had laid more of that road than anybody except Rell, spent two days on the route with him and drew a stretch of it in charcoal on the back of a board — the ground east of the pale stone, which Petch had never seen and Steadfast had, so that in the end Steadfast was dictating and Petch was drawing, and Petch marked the parts he was not sure of in a lighter hand without being told to.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast looked at that for a while and did not explain it to anybody either.</p>
<p class="p">Sal, who was fourteen and had a face like her mother's, asked him whether he was going to go and get his name.</p>
<p class="d">— I do not know, he said. — I think it might be waiting there.</p>
<p class="d">— That is where you left it.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes.</p>
<p class="p">The valley did not make an occasion of his going, which he had known they would not and was grateful for. He went out on the road at first light with his pack on, and Rell was at the north end because she was always at the north end at first light, and she said:</p>
<p class="d">— Your elbow held six winters.</p>
<p class="d">— It did.</p>
<p class="d">— It will hold six more. Somebody will have to do the mouth again in about four years and I will tell them you did it twice and got it wrong once, so they do not think it is easy.</p>
<p class="d">— Thank you.</p>
<p class="d">— Go on, then, said Rell.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">He walked up the made road, past the place where the sections change hands, past the old woman's four hundred yards that they had taken up and laid again, past Petch's two hundred that a boy of fifteen had put down with his own hands, out to the north end where it stopped at the well.</p>
<p class="p">He stood at the end of it.</p>
<p class="p">Then he stepped off the made ground onto the open, going east, the way Constant had gone, and did not look back at the valley, because he had spent six years there and knew exactly what it looked like, and because looking back would have put him wrong.</p>
<div class="partpage"><h1>PART FIVE</h1><h2>The Return</h2></div>
<h3 class="sec">Fifty-Five</h3>
<p class="p">It took him a hundred and four days and he was fifty-nine years old, and there were four occasions on it when he thought he had made a mistake that would kill him, and one of them nearly did.</p>
<p class="p">He will not be interesting about the journey. He had Petch's board and it was good for the first three weeks and then he was past where he had told Petch anything. He had Ivo's flask, which was better than the old one, and he took it out at a seam under a lip nine days west of the garden and held it up to the light at the angle, and did not laugh this time.</p>
<p class="p">He came at the country from the wrong side, which is to say from the side he had left by, and everything he had been told to look for he had been told to look for going the other way. The seam under the fourth rise took him two days to find because he was reading the ground reversed. The third fold he walked past twice.</p>
<p class="p">And then on the ninety-ninth day he came round a shoulder and there was a stand of burnt scrub on his right hand, and he stopped so fast he hurt his back.</p>
<p class="p">He stood and looked at it for a while, on the right, where it had always been on the left.</p>
<p class="p">Then he sat down on his pack in the middle of nowhere and put his hands over his face for a minute, and got up, and went on.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Fifty-Six</h3>
<p class="p">He smelled the smoke on the afternoon of the fourth day after that, and it frightened him, though by then there was nothing frightening about it.</p>
<p class="p">The garden had a second wall.</p>
<p class="p">That was the first thing, from the top of the fold. The old wall was there, waist high, the whole way round, four sides, and running out from the north-east corner of it was a second enclosure about half again the size with a lower wall and rows in it he did not recognise. There were more canes. There was a lean-to on the south side that had not existed, with a proper door on it, hung well.</p>
<p class="p">And there was a second building, small, thirty yards off, with its own chimney and smoke coming out of it.</p>
<p class="p">He stood on the fold for longer than he meant to.</p>
<p class="p">She was in the far rows and she had seen him. He could tell, because she had stopped, and had straightened up with a hand in the small of her back, and was standing there facing him, and had been for some time.</p>
<p class="p">He came down.</p>
<p class="p">She came out through the gap that was not a gap any more — there was a gate in it now, hung on two straps of leather, standing open — and walked out from her own wall onto the open ground, and stopped.</p>
<p class="p">Six feet.</p>
<p class="p">Twenty-one years of it, the distance she had put between herself and everyone who had ever come over that fold, and he knew the exact number of paces and he stopped at it out of pure habit, at the place, on his side of it, because that was what he had been taught by watching.</p>
<p class="p">And Grieve looked at him standing there at her own distance, and made a sound that was almost a laugh and was not, and crossed it, and got hold of him.</p>
<p class="p">She was not a large woman and she was sixty and she nearly had him over.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">They did not say anything for a while. Then she stood back and looked at him properly and said:</p>
<p class="d">— You are grey.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes.</p>
<p class="d">— Steadfast, you are —</p>
<p class="p">She stopped. She had said it in the ordinary way, the way she had said it four hundred times over two years, pass me that, the light is going, and it had come out of twenty-one years of habit before she had thought about it, and she heard it, and he watched her hear it.</p>
<p class="d">— No, she said. — Sorry.</p>
<p class="d">— It is all right.</p>
<p class="d">— It is not. I knew that before you left and I said it every day anyway.</p>
<p class="d">— You said it every day because I could not, said Steadfast. — I worked that out about four countries ago and I have wanted to tell you since.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve looked at him for a moment.</p>
<p class="d">— Well, she said. — Come in. There is somebody in the house and I do not want to leave him long.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Fifty-Seven</h3>
<p class="p">His name was Aldo and he was very old and he was not entirely there.</p>
<p class="p">He was in the house, in a chair by the stove that had been made for him — the joints were wrong, it was a chair somebody had built without much idea how — with a blanket over his knees, and he looked up when they came in and said something pleasant and slightly beside the point, and Grieve answered it as though it had been the point.</p>
<p class="p">She fed him. She fed him <em>by hand</em>, some of it, cutting things up small and putting them into his hand and putting his hand where he could find his mouth, and talking the whole time in a low continuous way that did not require answering.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast stood in the door of the house he had lived in for two years and watched a woman who had once let a girl walk east into nothing with a flask and a bundle because the girl had not asked her to stop.</p>
<p class="p">He got it, then, and it went through him like cold water.</p>
<p class="p">There had never been a method. He had spent seven years turning a distance and a silence into a doctrine, and building a whole practice out of it, and getting it wrong with a young man over a map. She had stood off from the man in the autumn because that man needed her to stand off. She had let Ash go because Ash was twenty-two and packed and going. She had fed Steadfast first and asked nothing because he had been four months out and could not have answered anything.</p>
<p class="p">And she was cutting an old man's food up small because that was what was in front of her now.</p>
<p class="d">— How long has he been here.</p>
<p class="d">— Two years in the spring. He came over the fold on his own feet and he could talk then. He is somebody's father and I do not know whose and neither does he now.</p>
<p class="d">— And you —</p>
<p class="d">— I built him a chair, Grieve said. — It is a bad chair. I am not Ivo.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast opened his mouth and shut it.</p>
<p class="d">— You do not know who Ivo is, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— No, said Grieve. — But you said it like I would.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Fifty-Eight</h3>
<p class="p">He told her on the second evening, out on the step, because he could not do it in the house.</p>
<p class="p">He had rehearsed it for a hundred and four days and every version had been wrong, and what came out in the end was:</p>
<p class="d">— There is a fire four days east of here.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve did not say anything.</p>
<p class="d">— On the hard ground where a cart can come within two days. There is a stone hut with water in it and dry wood and food in a sealed pit, and a brazier on the roof that whoever is living there keeps lit. Four households out of a valley eight hundred miles from here take it a year each. The first one has been in it a year and a half.</p>
<p class="p">Still nothing.</p>
<p class="d">— And there was already one, he said. — Forty miles north of where we built. A man and two women at a seam. Three years before we got there and they had never heard of anybody and neither had we.</p>
<p class="p">He made himself stop.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve sat with her hands round her cup and looked out at the dark where the Waste was, and did not move for a long time, and Steadfast, who had learned to let a silence run three beats past comfortable, let this one run as long as it wanted.</p>
<p class="d">— Say the number, she said at last.</p>
<p class="d">— What?</p>
<p class="d">— The number they have had. At the hut. Say it.</p>
<p class="d">— Nineteen, he said. — In eighteen months. The first household counted because I asked them to.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve put the cup down on the step.</p>
<p class="p">Then she put her hand over her mouth and made a sound he had never heard out of her and hoped never to hear again, and he did not move, and she got hold of the edge of the step with her other hand and held on to it and cried the way people cry who have been standing up for a very long time.</p>
<p class="p">He did not touch her. That was not restraint and it was not method. He simply looked at her and understood, correctly, that she was not crying about the nineteen.</p>
<p class="orn">§</p>
<p class="p">She said it herself, later, when she could.</p>
<p class="d">— I have thought about that number every night for twenty-one years, she said. — The one I do not have. The ones who came over that ground and did not come close enough, and I have made them up, I have made up faces, I have made up a boy of about fourteen and I have thought about him for eleven years and he does not exist, I invented him because the number needed a face.</p>
<p class="p">She wiped her eyes with the back of her wrist and left dirt there, and he could not look at her for a second.</p>
<p class="d">— And you are telling me some of them are nineteen.</p>
<p class="d">— Some of them are nineteen.</p>
<p class="d">— And some of them are still the other thing.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes, said Steadfast, because it was true and because she would have known if he had softened it.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes, said Grieve.</p>
<p class="p">She picked the cup back up.</p>
<p class="d">— Good, she said. — That is the right answer. If you had told me it was all of them I would have thrown you off the fold.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Fifty-Nine</h3>
<p class="p">He saw the wall on the third morning.</p>
<p class="p">He had walked it the first evening in the dark and had not looked, or had not let himself, and on the third morning he went out early because he always did and walked the whole four sides in the flat light with his hands behind his back.</p>
<p class="p">It was good. The north run had settled and been repointed. The gate was hung well. The second enclosure was newer work and had been done by more than one pair of hands, and he stood at the join for a while and could see exactly where the sections changed.</p>
<p class="p">And in the middle of the north run, in eighty feet of honest wall that had been taken up and relaid at least once since he left, there was a bulge.</p>
<p class="p">Two stones forced together that did not want to go, out of line with everything on either side of it, ugly as sin, load-bearing.</p>
<p class="p">She had taken the wall down and put it back up and left it in.</p>
<p class="p">He stood in front of it for a long time. Then he put his hand flat on it, the way he had put his hand flat on the grass twenty-two years earlier in a doorway a thousand miles east of there, and left it there a moment, and took it away.</p>
<p class="p">He did not ask her about it. She did not mention it. Neither of them ever did.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Sixty</h3>
<p class="p">The stranger asked on the fifth day and it was almost funny that it was him.</p>
<p class="p">There was a man passing — not stopping, a lean quick man who took food and would not sit down, going somewhere with his own reasons — and he was in the yard with his pack on and he said to Grieve, jerking his chin at Steadfast:</p>
<p class="d">— And him. What is he called.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast said: — I have not got one just now.</p>
<p class="p">He said it the way he had said it to an eight-year-old on a kerb, flatly, without any weight on it, and it was the fourth or fifth time he had said it in seven years and it had stopped being difficult.</p>
<p class="p">The lean man looked at him as though he were slightly simple.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve said, without looking up from what she was doing: — He is the ninth.</p>
<p class="p">The man waited for more, did not get it, decided it was not worth his trouble, and went.</p>
<p class="p">Steadfast stood in the yard.</p>
<p class="d">— The ninth, he said.</p>
<p class="d">— That is what you are. You are the ninth one who came over that fold and found it. I have had thirty-one now. You are still the ninth.</p>
<p class="p">She said it the way she said everything, as a fact about the arrangement of the world that she had been carrying so long she had stopped noticing the weight of it, and then she looked up and saw his face and stopped.</p>
<p class="d">— Is that wrong?</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— Because I can —</p>
<p class="d">— No, said Steadfast. — No. Leave it.</p>
<p class="p">And that was the whole of it, and it was not a ceremony, and nobody said anything else about it, and by the end of that week it was what she called him and it did not sound strange in her mouth at all.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Sixty-One</h3>
<p class="p">He stayed eleven weeks.</p>
<p class="p">He worked. Of course he worked. He did the gutter properly, with Ivo's four questions in his head and Ivo's drawing in his hand, and it was better than her gutter and she made him show her twice and then said, — Well, that is annoying, and used it.</p>
<p class="p">He took the old man out into the sun on the good days, chair and all, and sat with him, and had long conversations with him that were about four different things at once and did not need to be about anything.</p>
<p class="p">He built forty yards of a new wall on the west side of the second enclosure and did it properly, and Grieve watched him lay six stones without lifting any of them and said nothing at all, and went inside, and he heard the pot go on.</p>
<p class="p">And one evening she took a box out from under the shelf by the stove.</p>
<p class="p">It was wooden and about the size of two hands and it had a lid that fitted. She took it out, and sat with it on her knees in the chair by the fire for perhaps a quarter of an hour, and did not open it, and put it back.</p>
<p class="p">He did not ask. She did not offer. He thought about it for the rest of his life and never knew, and that was correct, and he knew it was correct at the time.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Sixty-Two</h3>
<p class="p">He said the other thing on the last evening, on the step, which was where she had refused to let him say it twenty-one years earlier.</p>
<p class="d">— You would not let me say it, he said. — On the step. The morning I went.</p>
<p class="d">— I remember.</p>
<p class="d">— I have carried it since.</p>
<p class="d">— I know you have, said Grieve. — That is why I would not let you. It would have come out wrong and you would have carried the wrong version, and I would rather you carried the true one for twenty years than the wrong one for one.</p>
<p class="d">— That is a very long game.</p>
<p class="d">— I am sixty years old and I live in a hole, she said. — Long games are what I have got.</p>
<p class="p">He laughed. Then he sat with it for a while, and she let him, and the light went orange the way it did an hour before it went.</p>
<p class="d">— It was not thank you, he said. — That is what I have worked out. I had thank you ready and it was not that.</p>
<p class="d">— No.</p>
<p class="d">— It was: I am sorry that I am going.</p>
<p class="p">He looked at his hands.</p>
<p class="d">— Not sorry that I went. I was right to go, and you were right that I had to have my own reason, and I found one eventually and it took about four years. Sorry that it was going to cost you and that I was going to do it anyway. Both of those at once. I did not have anywhere to put both of those at once and I have spent twenty-one years finding out that you can just — hold them.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve was quiet.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes, she said. — All right. That is worth twenty-one years.</p>
<p class="p">And then, after a while:</p>
<p class="d">— It did cost.</p>
<p class="d">— I know.</p>
<p class="d">— I am telling you because you have got old enough to hear it without turning it into a reason to stay. It cost. It cost for about two years, badly, and then it was just a thing that had happened.</p>
<p class="d">— Yes.</p>
<p class="d">— And I would not have had you stay, she said. — Both of those. You can hold them.</p>
<h3 class="sec">Sixty-Three</h3>
<p class="p">He went on a clear morning.</p>
<p class="p">She packed him two days of food, the same cloth, the same three things, and neither of them said anything about that either, and this time he laughed while she was doing it and she said — what — and he said — nothing — and she said — liar — and went on packing.</p>
<p class="d">— The seam under the fourth rise, she said.</p>
<p class="d">— I know.</p>
<p class="d">— And the burnt scrub —</p>
<p class="d">— On the left, going out. I know. I found it on the right coming in and I sat down on my pack.</p>
<p class="p">Grieve stopped what she was doing.</p>
<p class="d">— Did you.</p>
<p class="d">— For a minute.</p>
<p class="d">— Huh, she said.</p>
<p class="p">She put the bundle in his hands.</p>
<p class="d">— Do not drink the flask on the first day, no matter what you tell yourself about the second.</p>
<p class="d">— I know.</p>
<p class="d">— I know you know. I am saying it.</p>
<p class="p">He got the bundle into the pack. Aldo was asleep in his chair in the sun with the blanket over his knees and Steadfast had said goodbye to him already, twice, because he would not remember the first one and possibly not the second.</p>
<p class="p">At the gate Grieve said: — Are you coming back?</p>
<p class="p">He thought about it honestly, which she deserved.</p>
<p class="d">— I do not know, he said. — It is a hundred and four days and I am fifty-nine.</p>
<p class="d">— That is not a no.</p>
<p class="d">— It is not a no.</p>
<p class="d">— Good, said Grieve. — Then I will not tidy the lean-to.</p>
<p class="p">He went out through the gate and up onto the fold and at the top of it he turned round, which he had not done last time.</p>
<p class="p">She was standing in the gate.</p>
<p class="p">Not in the rows. Not with her back to him. Standing in the gate with her hand up against the post, watching him go, and when she saw him turn she lifted the hand off the post and held it up, and kept it there.</p>
<p class="p">He put his hand up.</p>
<p class="p">Then he went down the far side and the smoke went out of sight behind him about a quarter of an hour later, and he did not stop to look for it, because he knew exactly where it was, and because there was a hut with a brazier on it four days east, and a man and two women at a seam forty miles north of that, and none of them knew about each other yet, and he was the only person alive who had been in all three places.</p></body></html>